import { nativeOlympiadContent, p, h, sub, fig } from "./native-content";

// Source provenance and model corrections: docs/engineering/NATIVE_ARCHIVE_BATCH_9.md.

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2024-t1"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Geometry of Water Fountain"),
    p("A hemispherical fountain sprayer of radius \\(r\\) is at height \\(h\\) above horizontal ground. Its radius is small compared with the water range, so treat it as a point source. All holes eject water at speed \\(v_0\\), at elevation \\(0<\\theta<\\pi/2\\) above horizontal. There are \\(\\rho(\\theta)\\) holes per unit hemisphere area. Ignore air resistance and use gravitational acceleration \\(g\\). Part A assumes uniform hole density. Part B seeks uniform coverage on the ground."),
    fig("batch9-fountain", "Original hemispherical point sprayer, elevation theta, launch speed, height and water trajectories."),
    sub("A.1. Projectile position"),
    p("Find \\(x(t)\\) and \\(y(t)\\), with the ground at \\(y=0\\)."),
    sub("A.2. Water trajectory"),
    p("Eliminate time to obtain \\(y(x,\\theta)\\) using just one type of trigonometric function."),
    sub("A.3. Trajectory envelope"),
    p("Derive the minimal boundary enclosing all trajectories and sketch it over the trajectories."),
    sub("A.4. Landing range"),
    p("Find the positive landing range \\(R(\\theta)\\)."),
    sub("A.5. Zero-height range"),
    p("Show that as \\(h\\to0\\), \\(R=v_0^2\\sin(2\\theta)/g\\)."),
    sub("B.1. Ground annulus"),
    p("Express the elemental ground annulus area in terms of \\(R,R_{\\mathrm{prime}}=dR/d\\theta,d\\theta\\), taking \\(R_{\\mathrm{prime}}>0\\)."),
    sub("B.2. Hole density"),
    p("Determine the angular hole density up to an overall constant for uniform ground coverage. Use \\(R_{\\mathrm{prime}}>0\\)."),
    sub("B.3. Flat-source distribution"),
    p("For \\(h=0\\) and \\(0<\\theta<45^\\circ\\), find the explicit angular dependence for uniform coverage."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Geometry of Water Fountain"),
    sub("A.1. Projectile position"),
    p("Horizontal acceleration vanishes and vertical acceleration is \\(-g\\), hence \\[x=v_0t\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=h+v_0t\\sin\\theta-\\tfrac12gt^2.\\]"),
    sub("A.2. Water trajectory"),
    p("Substitute \\(t=x/(v_0\\cos\\theta)\\) and \\(\\sec^2\\theta=1+\\tan^2\\theta\\): \\[y=h+x\\tan\\theta-\\frac{gx^2}{2v_0^2}(1+\\tan^2\\theta).\\]"),
    sub("A.3. Trajectory envelope"),
    p("For fixed \\(x\\), maximize the preceding quadratic in \\(u=\\tan\\theta\\). The maximum occurs at \\(u=v_0^2/(gx)\\), giving \\[y_{\\rm env}=h+\\frac{v_0^2}{2g}-\\frac{gx^2}{2v_0^2}.\\] Equivalently its discriminant in \\(u\\) vanishes. Sketch this downward parabola tangent to every trajectory, with vertex at \\(x=0,y=h+v_0^2/(2g)\\)."),
    sub("A.4. Landing range"),
    p("Solve \\(y(t)=0\\) for the positive time, then multiply by \\(v_0\\cos\\theta\\): \\[R=\\frac{v_0\\cos\\theta}{g}\\left(v_0\\sin\\theta+\\sqrt{v_0^2\\sin^2\\theta+2gh}\\right).\\]"),
    sub("A.5. Zero-height range"),
    p("For \\(0<\\theta<\\pi/2\\), the square root becomes \\(v_0\\sin\\theta\\). Thus \\(R=2v_0^2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta/g=v_0^2\\sin(2\\theta)/g\\)."),
    sub("B.1. Ground annulus"),
    p("The annulus has area \\(dA_W=2\\pi R\\,dR=2\\pi RR_{\\mathrm{prime}}\\,d\\theta\\). If the angle branch decreases in range, use \\(|R_{\\mathrm{prime}}|\\)."),
    sub("B.2. Hole density"),
    p("The hemisphere band area is \\(2\\pi r^2\\cos\\theta\\,d\\theta\\). Equal water delivery per ground area requires \\(\\rho\\,2\\pi r^2\\cos\\theta\\,d\\theta\\propto2\\pi RR_{\\mathrm{prime}}\\,d\\theta\\), so \\[\\rho(\\theta)\\propto\\frac{RR_{\\mathrm{prime}}}{r^2\\cos\\theta}.\\]"),
    sub("B.3. Flat-source distribution"),
    p("With \\(R=v_0^2\\sin2\\theta/g\\) and \\(R_{\\mathrm{prime}}=2v_0^2\\cos2\\theta/g\\), \\[\\rho\\propto\\frac{v_0^4}{g^2r^2}\\frac{\\sin4\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}.\\] The requested angular shape is \\(\\sin4\\theta/\\cos\\theta\\). Restricting the angle avoids two launch angles delivering water to the same ring."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2024-t2"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Snell's Law"),
    p("At a dielectric interface, \\(n_1\\sin\\phi_1=n_2\\sin\\phi_2\\), with angles measured from its normal. Light travels at speed \\(c/n\\). Use geometrical optics and the original diagrams. Part A concerns a semicircular block in air. Part B concerns a cylindrical rod of index \\(n_1=1.50\\), surrounded by index \\(n_2=1.00\\), with an entry polymer of index \\(n_3=1.40\\). Part C concerns meridional rays in an optical fibre."),
    fig("batch9-snell", "Original interface normal, incident angle phi1 and transmitted angle phi2."),
    sub("A.1. Two colours"),
    fig("batch9-semicircle-rays", "Original two coloured rays entering the semicircle radially and refracting at its flat face."),
    p("Rays a and b enter a semicircular block radially and leave its flat bottom with refraction angles \\(\\theta_a<\\theta_b\\). Which ray travels faster inside? Explain."),
    sub("A.2. Index difference"),
    p("As internal incidence increases, ray b ceases to exit at \\(45^\\circ\\), and a at \\(50^\\circ\\). Find \\(n_b-n_a\\)."),
    sub("B.1. Return to the polymer"),
    fig("batch9-optical-rod", "Original polymer-coupled cylindrical rod with side and end reflections."),
    p("Light enters the rod from the polymer at angle \\(\\theta\\) to its axis. Determine the incident-angle range for total reflection at both the side and far end, returning light to the polymer."),
    sub("B.2.i. Oil at the far end"),
    p("What happens if the far end is coated with a thick oil layer of index 1.60?"),
    sub("B.2.ii. Immersion in water"),
    p("For the original rod without oil, what happens in water of index 1.33?"),
    sub("C.1. Straight-fibre acceptance"),
    fig("batch9-optical-fibre", "Original fibre core, cladding and meridional acceptance-ray geometry."),
    p("A fibre core of index \\(n_1\\) is surrounded by cladding of index \\(n_2<n_1\\). External medium has index \\(n_3\\). Find maximum acceptance angle \\(\\theta_a\\)."),
    sub("C.2. Bent-fibre acceptance"),
    p("The core diameter is \\(50\\,\\mu\\mathrm m\\) and the minimum bend radius of its centreline is 1.0 cm. Derive the altered acceptance angle for the limiting meridional ray."),
    sub("C.3. Acceptance angles in air"),
    p("For \\(n_1=1.45,n_2=1.44,n_3=1\\), find both acceptance angles using C.1 and C.2."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Snell's Law"),
    sub("A.1. Two colours"),
    p("At the common internal incidence angle, Snell gives \\(n_a<n_b\\). Thus \\(c/n_a>c/n_b\\), so ray a travels faster."),
    sub("A.2. Index difference"),
    p("At critical incidence \\(n\\sin\\theta_c=1\\). Therefore \\(n_b-n_a=1/\\sin45^\\circ-1/\\sin50^\\circ=0.1088\\)."),
    sub("B.1. Return to the polymer"),
    p("If the rod angle is \\(\\theta_1\\), side reflection requires \\(\\cos\\theta_1>n_2/n_1\\), whereas far-end reflection requires \\(\\sin\\theta_1>n_2/n_1\\). Using \\(n_3\\sin\\theta=n_1\\sin\\theta_1\\), \\[\\arcsin(n_2/n_3)<\\theta<\\arcsin(\\sqrt{n_1^2-n_2^2}/n_3).\\] Numerically \\(45.58^\\circ<\\theta<53.00^\\circ\\)."),
    sub("B.2.i. Oil at the far end"),
    p("The far-end medium has index greater than the rod. Total internal reflection there is impossible. No incident-angle interval returns all the light to the polymer."),
    sub("B.2.ii. Immersion in water"),
    p("Side reflection requires \\(\\theta_1<\\arccos(1.33/1.50)=27.54^\\circ\\), while far-end reflection requires \\(\\theta_1>\\arcsin(1.33/1.50)=62.46^\\circ\\). These intervals do not intersect, so no total-return interval exists."),
    sub("C.1. Straight-fibre acceptance"),
    p("At the side-wall critical angle, the core angle to the axis satisfies \\(\\sin\\theta_{\\rm core}=\\sqrt{n_1^2-n_2^2}/n_1\\). Entry refraction gives \\[\\sin\\theta_a=\\frac{\\sqrt{n_1^2-n_2^2}}{n_3}.\\] If this exceeds one, all physical entry angles are accepted."),
    sub("C.2. Bent-fibre acceptance"),
    p("Let \\(a=25\\,\\mu\\mathrm m\\), bend radius \\(R=10000\\,\\mu\\mathrm m\\). Conservation of the ray impact parameter between inner and outer core radii requires \\(\\sin\\phi_{\\rm inner}=(R+a)\\sin\\phi_{\\rm outer}/(R-a)\\). Set \\(\\sin\\phi_{\\rm outer}=n_2/n_1\\). Entry refraction then gives \\[\\sin\\theta_{a,\\rm bend}=\\frac1{n_3}\\sqrt{n_1^2-n_2^2\\left(\\frac{R+a}{R-a}\\right)^2}.\\] It is smaller than the straight value. If the radicand is negative, the limiting inner-edge ray cannot be guided."),
    sub("C.3. Acceptance angles in air"),
    p("The straight acceptance is \\(\\arcsin\\sqrt{1.45^2-1.44^2}=9.79^\\circ\\). The bent result is \\(\\arcsin\\sqrt{1.45^2-1.44^2(10025/9975)^2}=5.15^\\circ\\), about \\(4.64^\\circ\\) less. Use the centreline-radius convention of the source."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2016-t1"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Two Problems in Mechanics"),
    p("Part A: a wooden cylinder of radius \\(r_1\\), thickness \\(h_1\\), density \\(\\rho_1\\), and total mass \\(M\\) contains a metal disk replacing wood. Its density is \\(\\rho_2>\\rho_1\\), radius \\(r_2\\), thickness \\(h_2\\), and axis B is parallel to cylinder axis S at distance \\(d\\). The disk is midway between the end faces. Known quantities are \\(r_1,h_1,\\rho_1,\\rho_2,M\\). Let the centre of mass C be distance \\(b\\) from S. Part B: a wheel-shaped space station of radius \\(R\\) rotates at angular speed \\(\\omega_{ss}\\). Astronauts live on the inside rim. Ignore the station gravity and local floor curvature where specified. Earth has radius \\(R_E\\) and surface gravity \\(g_E\\)."),
    sub("A.1. Centre of mass offset"),
    fig("batch9-hidden-disk", "Original side and top views of the wooden cylinder containing an offset metal disk."),
    fig("batch9-tilted-cylinder", "Original cylinder resting on a tilted base with the source phi angle convention."),
    p("On a horizontal surface the cylinder is in stable equilibrium. Slowly incline the surface through \\(\\Theta\\). The cylinder rolls without slipping and reaches equilibrium after rotating through the measured angle \\(\\phi\\) shown. Determine \\(b\\)."),
    sub("A.2. Suspended-cylinder period"),
    fig("batch9-suspended-cylinder", "Original cylinder suspended about its symmetry axis and displaced from equilibrium."),
    p("Suspend the cylinder at S, displace it through a small angle \\(\\varphi\\), and measure period \\(T\\). Find the equation of motion and inertia \\(I_S\\)."),
    sub("A.3. Disk offset"),
    p("Express \\(d\\) in terms of \\(b\\) and known quantities, allowing \\(r_2,h_2\\)."),
    sub("A.4. Geometrical inertia"),
    p("Find \\(I_S\\) from geometry using \\(b\\), the known quantities, and optionally \\(r_2,h_2\\)."),
    sub("A.5. Hidden-disk dimensions"),
    p("Determine \\(r_2,h_2\\) in terms of the measured \\(b,T\\) and known quantities."),
    sub("B.1. Artificial gravity"),
    fig("batch9-station-wheel", "Original rotating wheel station with rim inhabitants and radius R."),
    p("Find the rotation rate producing gravity \\(g_E\\) at the rim."),
    sub("B.2. Earth spring oscillator"),
    p("A mass \\(m\\) on a spring of stiffness \\(k\\) moves only vertically. Find its angular frequency in uniform terrestrial gravity."),
    sub("B.3. Station spring oscillator"),
    p("Find the angular oscillation frequency when horizontal motion is constrained in the station."),
    sub("B.4. Terrestrial gravity gradient"),
    p("Include the first-order height dependence of Earth gravity. Find \\(g_E(h)\\) and angular oscillator frequency \\(\\widetilde\\omega_E\\). Ignore Earth rotation."),
    sub("B.5. Indistinguishable frequencies"),
    p("For which station radius do B.3 and B.4 coincide?"),
    sub("B.6. Small-height drop"),
    p("Drop a mass from height \\(H\\ll R\\). Assuming constant downward acceleration and \\(d_x\\ll H\\), find horizontal velocity and displacement at the floor. Either consistent horizontal sign convention is allowed."),
    sub("B.7. Return to the tower base"),
    p("A sufficiently tall tower allows the released mass to hit its base, with zero relative horizontal displacement. Find the smallest nonzero height for this to occur."),
    sub("B.8. Unconstrained oscillator trajectory"),
    fig("batch9-station-oscillator", "Original spring mounted on a horizontally sliding support and initial downward displacement."),
    p("The spring hangs from a ring free to slide horizontally. Let y point towards the station centre and pull the mass to \\(x=0,y=-d\\), then release at rest. Neglect Coriolis feedback on vertical motion. Find \\(x(t),y(t)\\), and sketch the trajectory, marking amplitudes and cusps."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Two Problems in Mechanics"),
    sub("A.1. Centre of mass offset"),
    p("The centre of mass lies vertically above the contact point, so the common horizontal offset satisfies \\(b\\sin\\phi=r_1\\sin\\Theta\\). Hence \\[b=r_1\\sin\\Theta/\\sin\\phi.\\]"),
    sub("A.2. Suspended-cylinder period"),
    p("The torque is \\(-Mgb\\sin\\varphi\\), so \\(I_S\\ddot\\varphi=-Mgb\\sin\\varphi\\simeq-Mgb\\varphi\\). Thus \\[I_S=MgbT^2/(4\\pi^2).\\]"),
    sub("A.3. Disk offset"),
    p("Define excess disk mass \\(\\Delta M=\\pi r_2^2h_2(\\rho_2-\\rho_1)=M-\\pi r_1^2h_1\\rho_1\\). The uniform wood contribution has centre S, so \\(Mb=\\Delta M d\\) and \\(d=Mb/\\Delta M\\)."),
    sub("A.4. Geometrical inertia"),
    p("The parallel-axis theorem gives \\[I_S=\\tfrac12\\pi h_1\\rho_1r_1^4+\\tfrac12\\Delta M r_2^2+\\frac{M^2b^2}{\\Delta M}.\\] Here \\(\\Delta M=M-\\pi r_1^2h_1\\rho_1\\)."),
    sub("A.5. Hidden-disk dimensions"),
    p("Use \\(I_S=MgbT^2/(4\\pi^2)\\) and the previous inertia: \\[r_2=\\sqrt{\\frac2{\\Delta M}\\left[\\frac{MgbT^2}{4\\pi^2}-\\frac{\\pi h_1\\rho_1r_1^4}{2}-\\frac{M^2b^2}{\\Delta M}\\right]},\\qquad h_2=\\frac{\\Delta M}{\\pi r_2^2(\\rho_2-\\rho_1)}.\\] These require a physically admissible positive radicand and a disk fitting inside the wood."),
    sub("B.1. Artificial gravity"),
    p("Equate \\(\\omega_{ss}^2R=g_E\\), giving \\(\\omega_{ss}=\\sqrt{g_E/R}\\)."),
    sub("B.2. Earth spring oscillator"),
    p("Gravity shifts equilibrium without changing stiffness. Thus \\(\\omega_E=\\sqrt{k/m}\\)."),
    sub("B.3. Station spring oscillator"),
    p("The centrifugal force varies linearly with radius. After shifting equilibrium, \\(m\\ddot y+(k-m\\omega_{ss}^2)y=0\\). Hence \\(\\omega=\\sqrt{k/m-\\omega_{ss}^2}\\), assuming positive effective stiffness."),
    sub("B.4. Terrestrial gravity gradient"),
    p("The downward magnitude is \\(g_E(h)=g_E(1+h/R_E)^{-2}\\simeq g_E(1-2h/R_E)\\). The upward variation reduces restoring stiffness, so \\[\\widetilde\\omega_E=\\sqrt{k/m-2g_E/R_E}.\\]"),
    sub("B.5. Indistinguishable frequencies"),
    p("Equate \\(g_E/R=2g_E/R_E\\), giving \\(R=R_E/2\\)."),
    sub("B.6. Small-height drop"),
    p("The fall time is \\(t_f=\\sqrt{2H/(\\omega_{ss}^2R)}\\). Integrate the Coriolis acceleration magnitude \\(2\\omega_{ss}g_Et\\): \\[|v_x|=2H\\omega_{ss},\\qquad |d_x|=\\frac13\\sqrt{\\frac{8H^3}{R}}.\\] Relative to the rotating floor the mass falls behind the tower for the usual forward-positive convention."),
    sub("B.7. Return to the tower base"),
    p("In the inertial frame the mass moves straight at speed \\(\\omega_{ss}(R-H)\\). Put \\(u=\\omega_{ss}t_f=\\sqrt{R^2-(R-H)^2}/(R-H)\\). Returning to the base requires \\(\\tan u=u\\) with the correct angular branch and \\(\\cos u>0\\). The first admissible nonzero root is \\(u=7.72525\\), between \\(2\\pi\\) and \\(5\\pi/2\\). Thus \\(H/R=1-1/\\sqrt{1+u^2}=0.87162\\). The root near 4.493 has negative cosine and does not describe a tower inside the rim."),
    sub("B.8. Unconstrained oscillator trajectory"),
    p("Write \\(\\omega=\\sqrt{k/m-\\omega_{ss}^2}\\). Then \\(y=-d\\cos\\omega t\\). Choosing \\(\\ddot x=-2\\omega_{ss}\\dot y\\), integrate twice with zero initial velocity: \\[x=\\frac{2\\omega_{ss}d}{\\omega}\\sin\\omega t-2\\omega_{ss}dt.\\] The opposite horizontal sign is equivalent. The path has vertical extrema \\(\\pm d\\), a drift, and cusps at \\(y=-d\\), separated horizontally by \\(4\\pi\\omega_{ss}d/\\omega\\). The source prints an inconsistent sign for \\(\\dot y\\). Differentiating the stated \\(y(t)\\) and using a single Coriolis convention resolves it."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2024-t3"] = {
  statement: [
    h("The First Discovered Quasar"),
    p("This problem studies the radio source 3C 273. The Moon has sidereal period 27.3 days and angular diameter 30 arcminutes. Use \\(3600\\) arcseconds per degree, \\(1\\,\\mathrm{pc}=3.09\\times10^{16}\\,\\mathrm m\\), \\(H=75\\,\\mathrm{km\\,s^{-1}\\,Mpc^{-1}}\\), \\(c\\simeq3.00\\times10^8\\,\\mathrm{m/s}\\), \\(L_\\odot=3.82\\times10^{26}\\,\\mathrm W\\), \\(M_\\odot=2\\times10^{30}\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\), and \\(\\mu_0=4\\pi\\times10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{T\\,m/A}\\). The original occultation, spectrum and radio-image figures provide the observational data."),
    sub("A.1. Lunar angular motion"),
    p("Find the lunar motion against background stars in one hour, one minute, and one second. Express each in degrees, arcminutes, and arcseconds."),
    sub("A.2. One lunar diameter"),
    p("How long does the Moon take to move by its own angular diameter?"),
    sub("B.1. Occultation oscillations"),
    fig("batch9-quasar-occultation", "Original 410 MHz disappearance and reappearance data, time direction and lunar-limb projections of components A and B."),
    p("What causes the intensity oscillations? Options: A, telescope diffraction pattern. B, mount instability. C, lunar surface features. D, atmosphere. Explain your choice."),
    sub("B.2. Component reappearance"),
    p("Why does component B not produce a separate bump at reappearance? Options: A, both orbit the Moon. B, changed line of sight. C, both align with the lunar limb at reappearance. D, one fades intrinsically. Explain what this reveals about source structure."),
    sub("B.3. Angular component separation"),
    p("Estimate the projected angular separation from the occultation graph and the lunar angular speed."),
    sub("C.1. Spectral redshift"),
    fig("batch9-quasar-spectrum", "Original 3C 273 optical spectrum and laboratory Balmer comparison with wavelength scale."),
    p("Compare corresponding Balmer-line wavelengths in the laboratory and 3C 273 spectra. Estimate \\(z=(\\lambda-\\lambda_0)/\\lambda_0\\)."),
    sub("C.2. Gravitational-redshift hypothesis"),
    p("If \\(z=(1-2GM/(c^2r))^{-1/2}-1\\), find the required mass for a source at the edge of the Galaxy, \\(r\\sim3\\times10^{21}\\,\\mathrm m\\), and at the edge of the Solar System, \\(r\\sim1.5\\times10^{13}\\,\\mathrm m\\). Explain why these are implausible."),
    sub("C.3. Cosmological distance"),
    p("Instead use \\(z\\simeq v/c\\) and Hubble law \\(v=Hd\\). Find the distance and compare it with the Galaxy."),
    sub("D.1. Spectral luminosity"),
    p("The flux is \\(F_\\nu=25000\\nu^{-0.3}\\,\\mathrm{Jy}\\), with frequency in Hz and \\(1\\,\\mathrm{Jy}=10^{-26}\\,\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-2}\\,Hz^{-1}}\\). Find \\(L_\\nu=4\\pi d^2F_\\nu\\)."),
    sub("D.2. Integrated radio luminosity"),
    p("Integrate over \\(10^7<\\nu<10^{11}\\,\\mathrm{Hz}\\)."),
    sub("D.3. Luminosity comparison"),
    p("Compare with the stated solar luminosity and \\(L_{MW}=1.5\\times10^{10}L_\\odot\\)."),
    sub("E.1. Annihilation photon energy"),
    p("Explain why electron-positron annihilation \\(e^++e^-\\to2\\gamma\\) at rest does not directly produce the observed optical and radio emission. Estimate photon energy and wavelength."),
    sub("E.2. Accretion power"),
    p("One solar mass falls from infinity to Schwarzschild radius \\(R_s=2GM/c^2\\) each year. Estimate Newtonian potential-energy power and compare it with D."),
    sub("F.1. Minimum-energy ratio"),
    fig("batch9-quasar-jet", "Original MERLIN radio image showing the bright compact core and extended jet."),
    p("For particle energy \\(U_e\\propto B^{-3/2}\\) and field energy \\(U_B\\propto B^2\\), minimize \\(U=U_e+U_B\\) at fixed volume and show \\(U_e/U_B=4/3\\)."),
    sub("F.2. Jet magnetic field"),
    p("Take the jet energy to be half the energy radiated in one second in D.2, and volume \\(10^{45}\\,\\mathrm{m^3}\\). Estimate its magnetic field."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("The First Discovered Quasar"),
    sub("A.1. Lunar angular motion"),
    p("The angular rate is \\(360^\\circ/(27.3\\times24\\,\\mathrm h)\\). In one hour: \\(0.54945^\\circ=32.967\\) arcmin \\(=1978.02\\) arcsec. In one minute: \\(0.0091575^\\circ=0.54945\\) arcmin \\(=32.967\\) arcsec. In one second: \\(0.000152625^\\circ=0.0091575\\) arcmin \\(=0.54945\\) arcsec. The source solution labels its final per-second result in degrees where arcseconds are intended."),
    sub("A.2. One lunar diameter"),
    p("Divide \\(30\\) arcminutes by \\(0.54945\\) arcminutes per minute to obtain \\(54.6\\,\\mathrm{min}\\)."),
    sub("B.1. Occultation oscillations"),
    p("The published key selects A. Physically, lunar occultation fringes are Fresnel diffraction at the lunar limb, recorded by the telescope. They should not be attributed to the telescope aperture alone. The other proposed instrumental and surface explanations do not account for the coherent occultation fringes. This wording discrepancy is retained for self-review."),
    sub("B.2. Component reappearance"),
    p("C. The two components can have different projected separations normal to the lunar limb at disappearance and reappearance. Their reappearance times coincide because their positions align along the local limb. This constrains the two-dimensional structure."),
    sub("B.3. Angular component separation"),
    p("The disappearance times differ by roughly half a minute. Multiply by \\(32.97\\) arcseconds per minute to obtain about \\(16.5\\) arcseconds, conventionally estimated as \\(15\\) arcseconds. Graph reading and projection limit precision."),
    sub("C.1. Spectral redshift"),
    p("For example, the rest H-gamma line at \\(434\\,\\mathrm{nm}\\) appears near \\(500\\,\\mathrm{nm}\\). Corresponding line ratios are approximately \\(1.158\\), giving \\(z\\simeq0.158\\). Accept reasonable estimates from several lines."),
    sub("C.2. Gravitational-redshift hypothesis"),
    p("Solve for \\[M=\\frac{c^2r}{2G}\\left[1-(1+z)^{-2}\\right].\\] At \\(z=0.158\\), this gives about \\(2.6\\times10^{17}M_\\odot\\) and \\(1.3\\times10^9M_\\odot\\), respectively. Either would dominate and severely disrupt the containing Galaxy or Solar System."),
    sub("C.3. Cosmological distance"),
    p("\\(d=zc/H=0.158\\times300000/75=632\\,\\mathrm{Mpc}\\), thousands of times the Galactic size. This is the stated low-redshift approximation, without a modern cosmological-distance correction."),
    sub("D.1. Spectral luminosity"),
    p("Using \\(d=632\\,\\mathrm{Mpc}=1.953\\times10^{25}\\,\\mathrm m\\), \\[L_\\nu=1.198\\times10^{30}\\nu^{-0.3}\\,\\mathrm{W/Hz}.\\] Rounding \\(d\\) to \\(2\\times10^{25}\\,\\mathrm m\\) gives the published coefficient \\(1.25\\times10^{30}\\). The luminosity has units W/Hz, not the flux units printed in the answer sheet."),
    sub("D.2. Integrated radio luminosity"),
    p("\\[L=\\frac{1.198\\times10^{30}}{0.7}\\left[(10^{11})^{0.7}-(10^7)^{0.7}\\right]\\simeq8.56\\times10^{37}\\,\\mathrm W.\\] The rounded-distance convention gives \\(8.94\\times10^{37}\\,\\mathrm W\\), conventionally \\(9\\times10^{37}\\,\\mathrm W\\)."),
    sub("D.3. Luminosity comparison"),
    p("The supplied benchmarks give \\(L/L_\\odot\\simeq(2.2\\text{ to }2.4)\\times10^{11}\\), and \\(L/L_{MW}\\simeq15\\). These are comparisons using the given luminosity benchmarks. The solar value is a bolometric luminosity, so it should not be described as the actual solar radio-band luminosity."),
    sub("E.1. Annihilation photon energy"),
    p("Each photon has \\(E=m_ec^2=0.511\\,\\mathrm{MeV}=8.19\\times10^{-14}\\,\\mathrm J\\), and \\(\\lambda=h/(m_ec)=2.43\\times10^{-12}\\,\\mathrm m=0.00243\\,\\mathrm{nm}\\). This is gamma radiation, far above optical and radio frequencies. The source sheet has an incorrect wavelength conversion and calls it X-rays. Reprocessing is outside this simple direct-emission comparison."),
    sub("E.2. Accretion power"),
    p("The model releases \\(GM\\dot M/R_s=\\dot Mc^2/2\\). With \\(\\dot M=2\\times10^{30}\\,\\mathrm{kg/yr}\\), \\(P=2.85\\times10^{39}\\,\\mathrm W\\), enough to supply the radio luminosity even with losses. The source numerical value uses \\(\\dot Mc^2\\), dropping its own factor one-half. This estimate is a Newtonian energy scale, not a relativistic accretion-efficiency calculation."),
    sub("F.1. Minimum-energy ratio"),
    p("At the stationary point \\(dU/dB=(-3U_e/2+2U_B)/B=0\\), so \\(U_e=4U_B/3\\). It is a minimum because both energy contributions grow at opposite ends of the positive B range."),
    sub("F.2. Jet magnetic field"),
    p("Use energy, not power: \\(U\\simeq4.3\\text{ to }4.5\\times10^{37}\\,\\mathrm J\\). From \\(U=7U_B/3\\), \\(U_B=3U/7\\), so \\[B=\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\mu_0(3U/7)}{V}}\\simeq2.2\\times10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm T.\\] The source rounds the luminosity to \\(10^{38}\\,\\mathrm W\\), yielding \\(2.3\\times10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm T\\)."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2017-t2"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Earthquake, Volcano and Tsunami"),
    p("Part A models the 2010 Merapi eruption as instantaneous, constant-volume mixing of magma mass \\(m_m\\) at \\(T_m\\) and water mass \\(m_w\\) at \\(T_w\\), followed by expansion. Neglect latent heats. Specific constant-volume heat capacities are \\(c_{Vm},c_{Vw}\\). Part B studies the 2006 Yogyakarta earthquake, origin time 22:54:00 UTC and hypocentre 15 km deep. The YOGI and DNP epicentral distances are 22.5 km and 500 km. The crust is 30 km deep above faster mantle. Ignore Earth curvature. Part C studies a tsunami generated by ocean-floor uplift, with density \\(\\rho\\) and local ocean depth \\(d\\)."),
    sub("A.1. Mixture equilibrium temperature"),
    p("Find the temperature after the first, constant-volume mixing stage."),
    sub("A.2. Mixture equilibrium pressure"),
    p("Model the mixture as ideal gas with molar volume \\(v_e\\). Find its equilibrium pressure."),
    sub("A.3. Eruption velocity scaling"),
    p("Find the gas-eruption velocity in terms of pressure \\(p\\), total mass \\(m\\), volume \\(V\\), and an unknown dimensionless factor \\(K\\)."),
    sub("B.1. Crust P-wave velocity"),
    fig("batch9-yogi-seismogram", "Original YOGI map and seismogram with labelled UTC time axis."),
    p("Read the P-wave arrival at YOGI from the seismogram and determine crust speed."),
    sub("B.2. Direct and reflected arrivals"),
    p("Find DNP travel times for the direct crustal ray and the ray reflected at the crust-mantle boundary."),
    sub("B.3. Mantle velocity"),
    fig("batch9-dnp-seismogram", "Original DNP map and seismogram with first P arrival near 22:55:15 UTC."),
    p("The earliest DNP arrival is about 22:55:15. Use a critically refracted head wave along the boundary to find mantle speed."),
    sub("B.4. Continuously varying crust"),
    p("Approximate the source at the surface and let \\(v(z)=v_0+az\\), \\(a>0\\). The ray parameter is \\(p=\\sin\\theta(z)/v(z)\\), with angle from the vertical. Find epicentral distance X."),
    sub("B.5. Travel-time integral"),
    p("Express the travel time as an integral over depth."),
    sub("B.6. Three-layer travel time"),
    fig("batch9-seismic-layers", "Original three-layer seismic-speed approximation and layer thickness labels."),
    p("Approximate the integral with three layers: \\(v_1=6.65,v_2=6.97,v_3=6.99\\,\\mathrm{km/s}\\), \\(p=0.143\\,\\mathrm{s/km}\\), and thicknesses \\(\\delta z_1=6,\\delta z_2=9,\\delta z_3=15\\,\\mathrm{km}\\)."),
    sub("C.1. Uplift potential energy"),
    fig("batch9-tsunami-uplift", "Original raised water box, wavelength half-length, alongshore extent and ocean depth."),
    p("A raised water box has area \\(\\lambda L/2\\), with \\(L\\gg\\lambda\\), and height \\(h\\). Find its gravitational energy above the original ocean surface."),
    sub("C.2. Shallow-water wave speed"),
    p("Use energy and mass conservation to obtain tsunami speed up to a dimensionless factor."),
    sub("C.3. Shoaling amplitude"),
    p("The depth changes slowly. At depth \\(d_0\\), amplitude is \\(A_0\\). Use energy conservation to find amplitude \\(A(d)\\)."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Earthquake, Volcano and Tsunami"),
    sub("A.1. Mixture equilibrium temperature"),
    p("Conservation of internal energy gives \\(m_wc_{Vw}(T_e-T_w)+m_mc_{Vm}(T_e-T_m)=0\\), hence \\[T_e=\\frac{m_wc_{Vw}T_w+m_mc_{Vm}T_m}{m_wc_{Vw}+m_mc_{Vm}}.\\]"),
    sub("A.2. Mixture equilibrium pressure"),
    p("The ideal-gas law gives \\(p_e=RT_e/v_e\\), with universal gas constant R."),
    sub("A.3. Eruption velocity scaling"),
    p("Pressure times volume is energy. Dimensional balance therefore requires \\(u=K\\sqrt{pV/m}\\)."),
    sub("B.1. Crust P-wave velocity"),
    p("The path is \\(\\sqrt{22.5^2+15^2}=27.04\\,\\mathrm{km}\\). The first arrival is about 4.7 s after origin, giving \\(v_c\\simeq5.75\\,\\mathrm{km/s}\\). Arrival picks around 4.5 to 5.5 s give roughly 4.9 to 6.0 km/s."),
    sub("B.2. Direct and reflected arrivals"),
    p("The direct distance is \\(\\sqrt{500^2+15^2}=500.225\\,\\mathrm{km}\\). Reflect the source in the boundary to obtain reflected distance \\(\\sqrt{500^2+45^2}=502.021\\,\\mathrm{km}\\). Divide by the same crust speed. At 5.75 km/s the times are 87.00 s and 87.31 s, respectively. Small differences from published rounded times reflect the graph-derived crust speed."),
    sub("B.3. Mantle velocity"),
    p("The crustal vertical travel totals \\(2\\times30-15=45\\,\\mathrm{km}\\). If \\(\\sin i=v_c/v_m\\), the time is \\[75=500/v_m+45\\sqrt{v_c^{-2}-v_m^{-2}}.\\] Solve with \\(v_m>v_c\\), obtaining approximately \\(v_m=7.1\\,\\mathrm{km/s}\\)."),
    sub("B.4. Continuously varying crust"),
    p("The turning depth obeys \\(p(v_0+az_t)=1\\). Along a ray \\(dx/dz=pv/\\sqrt{1-p^2v^2}\\). Thus \\[X=2\\int_0^{z_t}\\frac{p(v_0+az)}{\\sqrt{1-p^2(v_0+az)^2}}dz=\\frac2{ap}\\sqrt{1-p^2v_0^2}.\\]"),
    sub("B.5. Travel-time integral"),
    p("Use \\(dt=ds/v\\), \\(ds=dz/\\cos\\theta\\): \\[T(p)=2\\int_0^{z_t}\\frac{dz}{v(z)\\sqrt{1-p^2v(z)^2}}.\\] With slowness \\(u=1/v\\), the integrand is \\(u^2/\\sqrt{u^2-p^2}\\)."),
    sub("B.6. Three-layer travel time"),
    p("Calculate \\[T=2\\sum_{i=1}^3\\frac{\\delta z_i}{v_i\\sqrt{1-p^2v_i^2}}.\\] Direct evaluation of the supplied speeds gives about 184.1 s. The source first rounds the slownesses to 0.1504, 0.1435 and 0.1431 s/km and obtains 151.64 s. Near the turning point the cancellation makes early rounding significant. Both conventions must be explained rather than treating the source figure as uniquely precise."),
    sub("C.1. Uplift potential energy"),
    p("The raised mass is \\(\\rho\\lambda Lh/2\\), with centre at height \\(h/2\\). Hence \\(E_p=\\rho g\\lambda Lh^2/4\\)."),
    sub("C.2. Shallow-water wave speed"),
    p("Mass continuity gives horizontal water speed \\(U\\sim hv/d\\). Equate potential energy \\(\\sim\\rho g\\lambda Lh^2\\) and kinetic energy \\(\\sim\\rho d\\lambda LU^2\\). The result is \\(v\\sim\\sqrt{gd}\\), the shallow-water speed."),
    sub("C.3. Shoaling amplitude"),
    p("Energy per horizontal area scales as \\(A^2\\). Conservation of energy flux gives \\(A^2\\sqrt{gd}=A_0^2\\sqrt{gd_0}\\), and therefore \\[A=A_0(d_0/d)^{1/4}.\\] This assumes no dissipation, reflection, breaking or lateral focusing."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2017-t1"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Vortices in Superfluid"),
    p("At zero temperature superfluid helium is incompressible with density \\(\\rho\\). Its flow is irrotational except along thin vortex filaments of core radius \\(a\\). A closed path linking one filament has circulation \\(\\pm2\\pi\\kappa\\), where \\(\\kappa=\\hbar/m_{He}\\). A contractible path has zero circulation. Velocity fields superpose like magnetic fields of current-carrying wires, and each filament moves with the local flow due to the other filaments. Neglect the core contribution to energy and assume all relevant logarithms are large. A circular vortex loop of radius R has energy \\(U\\simeq2\\pi^2\\rho\\kappa^2R\\ln(R/a)\\) and momentum \\(P=2\\pi^2\\rho\\kappa R^2\\), normal to its plane by the circulation right-hand rule. In all questions draw directions as well as giving requested magnitudes. Solid walls impose zero normal flow."),
    fig("batch9-vortex-circulation", "Original linking and nonlinking loops around a vortex filament."),
    sub("A.1. Straight-filament flow"),
    fig("batch9-vortex-beaker", "Original straight vortex along the central axis of a cylindrical helium beaker."),
    p("A straight vertical vortex lies on the axis of a beaker of radius \\(R_0\\gg a\\). Draw streamlines and find velocity at radius r."),
    sub("A.2. Single-vortex surface"),
    p("Find the free-surface height profile, neglecting surface tension."),
    sub("B.1. Equal-circulation pair"),
    fig("batch9-opposite-vortices", "Original parallel opposite-circulation vortices translating as a pair."),
    fig("batch9-equal-vortices", "Original parallel equal-circulation vortices."),
    p("Two identical straight vortices are initially separated by \\(r_0\\). Find their initial velocities and trajectories."),
    sub("B.2. Lattice trajectories"),
    fig("batch9-vortex-lattice", "Original triangular vortex lattice, spacing u, outer beaker and marked vortices A, B and C."),
    p("A beaker contains a triangular lattice of identical vortices with spacing \\(u\\ll R_0\\). Draw the trajectories of A, B and the central vortex C."),
    sub("B.3. Lattice velocity"),
    p("Find the coarse-grained speed of a vortex at radial distance r."),
    sub("B.4. Lattice spacing in time"),
    p("Find \\(AB(t)\\), given \\(AB(0)\\)."),
    sub("B.5. Smoothed lattice surface"),
    p("Find the free surface after smoothing out individual vortex cores."),
    sub("C.1. Rectangular-loop momentum"),
    fig("batch9-vortex-ring-flow", "Original vortex ring and momentum-integration paths."),
    fig("batch9-rectangular-vortex", "Original long rectangular vortex loop with dimensions b and d."),
    p("A nearly rectangular vortex loop has dimensions \\(b\\times d\\), with \\(a\\ll b\\ll d\\). Find momentum and its direction."),
    sub("C.2. Rectangular-loop energy"),
    p("Find its leading-logarithm energy."),
    sub("C.3. Shifted-filament momentum"),
    fig("batch9-vortex-shift", "Original shifted filament with oriented rectangular difference loop and x-y axes."),
    p("A filament of length d shifts by b along x. Find the change of fluid momentum and its direction."),
    sub("D.1. Charged straight filament"),
    fig("batch9-charged-vortex", "Original charged straight filament in an electric field and axis convention."),
    p("A straight vortex has charge per length \\(\\lambda<0\\) and is placed in uniform transverse electric field E. Find trajectory and velocity."),
    sub("D.2. Charged-ring radius"),
    fig("batch9-charged-ring", "Original charged circular vortex ring and electric-field orientation."),
    p("A circular loop initially has radius \\(R_0\\) and uniform linear charge \\(\\lambda<0\\). Uniform E is normal to its plane and opposite its initial momentum. Draw the centre path and find R(t)."),
    sub("D.3. Charged-ring velocity"),
    p("Find the loop speed as a function of time in the leading-logarithm approximation."),
    sub("D.4. Field switched off"),
    p("At time \\(t_*\\), E is switched off and speed is \\(v_*\\). Find later speed."),
    sub("E.1. One flat wall"),
    fig("batch9-vortex-wall", "Original straight vortex at initial height h0 over a wall."),
    p("A straight vortex is initially distance \\(h_0\\) from a flat impermeable wall. Draw its trajectory and find its velocity."),
    sub("E.2. Right-angle corner"),
    fig("batch9-vortex-corner", "Original vortex in a right-angle corner with both initial distances h0."),
    p("A straight vortex is distance \\(h_0\\) from each of two perpendicular walls. Find its initial velocity."),
    sub("E.3. Corner trajectory"),
    p("Draw the vortex trajectory and direction."),
    sub("E.4. Long-time corner speed"),
    p("Find the speed after a long time."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Vortices in Superfluid"),
    sub("A.1. Straight-filament flow"),
    p("Symmetry gives concentric circles. Circulation \\(2\\pi rv=2\\pi\\kappa\\) implies \\(v=\\kappa/r\\), directed according to the circulation."),
    sub("A.2. Single-vortex surface"),
    p("The free-surface slope obeys \\(g\\,dz/dr=v^2/r=\\kappa^2/r^3\\). Integrating, \\[z(r)=z_0-\\kappa^2/(2gr^2).\\] The constant follows from liquid volume and the formula excludes the core."),
    sub("B.1. Equal-circulation pair"),
    p("Each moves at \\(\\kappa/r_0\\) perpendicular to their connecting line. They orbit their midpoint on opposite semicircles of radius \\(r_0/2\\), with common angular speed \\(2\\kappa/r_0^2\\). By contrast, opposite-circulation vortices translate together."),
    sub("B.2. Lattice trajectories"),
    p("The coarse-grained lattice rotates rigidly. A and B trace circles about the beaker centre. The central vortex C stays at the centre."),
    sub("B.3. Lattice velocity"),
    p("The number per area is \\(2/(\\sqrt3u^2)\\). Summing circulation inside a circle gives \\(2\\pi rv=2\\pi\\kappa\\pi r^2\\,2/(\\sqrt3u^2)\\), so \\[v=2\\pi\\kappa r/(\\sqrt3u^2).\\]"),
    sub("B.4. Lattice spacing in time"),
    p("The angular velocity \\(2\\pi\\kappa/(\\sqrt3u^2)\\) is independent of radius. Rigid rotation preserves separation: \\(AB(t)=AB(0)\\)."),
    sub("B.5. Smoothed lattice surface"),
    p("Rigid-body angular speed is \\(\\Omega=2\\pi\\kappa/(\\sqrt3u^2)\\). Integrating \\(g\\,dz/dr=\\Omega^2r\\) gives \\[z=z_0+2\\pi^2\\kappa^2r^2/(3gu^4).\\]"),
    sub("C.1. Rectangular-loop momentum"),
    p("Circulation times oriented area times density gives \\(P=2\\pi\\kappa\\rho bd\\), normal to the loop by the circulation right-hand rule."),
    sub("C.2. Rectangular-loop energy"),
    p("The two long opposite filaments each contribute kinetic energy. Integrating from core a to separation b, or using the two-wire inductance analogue, gives \\[U=2\\pi\\rho\\kappa^2d\\ln(b/a).\\] End corrections are smaller for \\(b\\ll d\\)."),
    sub("C.3. Shifted-filament momentum"),
    p("The difference between old and new flow is the flow of a rectangular loop. Thus \\(|\\Delta P|=2\\pi\\kappa\\rho bd\\), perpendicular to both the filament and its displacement. Its sign is fixed by the oriented loop formed by the new filament and the negative old filament."),
    sub("D.1. Charged straight filament"),
    p("For length d, electric force is \\(\\lambda d\\mathbf E\\). Momentum changes perpendicular to filament displacement, so the constant drift is perpendicular to E, with magnitude \\(|\\lambda|E/(2\\pi\\kappa\\rho)\\). Choose the sign using C.3. The path is a straight line, with no ordinary mass-times-acceleration law for the vortex."),
    sub("D.2. Charged-ring radius"),
    p("Total charge is fixed at \\(-2\\pi R_0|\\lambda|\\), so force is constant and parallel to initial momentum. From \\(P=P_0+2\\pi ER_0|\\lambda|t\\), \\[R(t)=\\sqrt{R_0^2+ER_0|\\lambda|t/(\\pi\\rho\\kappa)}.\\] The centre moves along the ring axis in the force direction. Charge density decreases as the ring grows."),
    sub("D.3. Charged-ring velocity"),
    p("Work and momentum balance imply \\(v=dU/dP\\). With the given U and P, \\[v\\simeq\\frac{\\kappa}{2R(t)}\\ln\\frac{R(t)}a.\\] Differentiating the approximate energy exactly adds a 1 inside the logarithmic bracket, which is beyond leading-logarithm accuracy. As R grows the speed decreases in the large-log regime. Replacing the slowly varying logarithm by \\(\\ln(R_0/a)\\) gives the further approximation in the source."),
    sub("D.4. Field switched off"),
    p("With no external force momentum stays constant, hence radius and speed stay constant: \\(v(t)=v_*\\) for \\(t>t_*\\)."),
    sub("E.1. One flat wall"),
    p("An opposite-circulation image across the wall enforces zero normal flow. Its distance is \\(2h_0\\). The vortex translates parallel to the wall at constant speed \\(\\kappa/(2h_0)\\), with direction fixed by the image circulation."),
    sub("E.2. Right-angle corner"),
    p("Use three images: opposite circulation across each wall and equal circulation across both. At \\((h_0,h_0)\\) the net components are opposite with magnitudes \\(\\kappa/(4h_0)\\). The speed is \\(\\kappa/(2\\sqrt2h_0)\\), tangent to the corner trajectory."),
    sub("E.3. Corner trajectory"),
    p("Energy conservation for the four-image arrangement implies \\[(x^2+y^2)/(x^2y^2)=2/h_0^2.\\] The curve passes through \\((h_0,h_0)\\), tangent to the direction with opposite x and y components. It approaches one wall at a fixed distance as it travels far along it, with direction set by the circulation."),
    sub("E.4. Long-time corner speed"),
    p("For \\(x\\to\\infty\\), the trajectory invariant yields \\(y\\to h_0/\\sqrt2\\). Only the nearest-wall image matters, giving \\[v_\\infty=\\kappa/(2y)=\\kappa/(\\sqrt2h_0).\\]"),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2022-t2"] = {
  statement: [
    h("A Mechanical Model for Phase Transitions"),
    p("A bead of mass m slides on a ring of radius R rotating about its vertical diameter at angular speed \\(\\omega\\). Angle \\(\\theta\\) is measured from the downward vertical, with \\(-\\pi/2<\\theta<\\pi/2\\). Let \\(\\omega_c=\\sqrt{g/R}\\). In the stated rotating-frame model, the only forces are gravity, the radial normal N, centrifugal force and opposing tangential force \\(-fkN\\), with \\(f=\\operatorname{sgn}\\dot\\theta\\). Use this simplified force diagram, omitting any additional forces. For B.1 through B.9 set k=0. Landau free energy has the form \\(V(\\mathcal M)=a(T)\\mathcal M^2+b(T)\\mathcal M^4\\), with b positive and a changing sign at the transition. Expansions \\(\\sin\\theta=\\theta-\\theta^3/6+\\cdots\\), \\(\\cos\\theta=1-\\theta^2/2+\\theta^4/24+\\cdots\\) may be used."),
    fig("batch9-rotating-bead", "Original ring rotating about its vertical diameter with the bead angle measured from the bottom."),
    fig("batch9-bead-forces", "Original stipulated rotating-frame normal, gravity, opposing tangential force and centrifugal force."),
    sub("A.1. Radial and tangential equations"),
    p("Write both equations of motion, with angle increasing counterclockwise."),
    sub("B.1. Equilibrium branches"),
    p("Find equilibrium angles in terms of \\(\\omega,\\omega_c\\)."),
    sub("B.2. Equilibrium-angle plot"),
    p("Sketch equilibrium angles against \\(\\omega/\\omega_c\\)."),
    sub("B.3. Normal-reaction plot"),
    p("Sketch normal-reaction magnitude at stable equilibrium against the rotation-rate ratio."),
    sub("B.4. Effective-potential coefficients"),
    p("With \\(F_\\theta=-R^{-1}dV/d\\theta\\), \\(V(0)=0\\), and \\(V=P+Q\\cos\\theta+S\\sin^2\\theta\\), determine P,Q,S."),
    sub("B.5. Quartic expansion"),
    p("Find \\(a(\\omega),b(\\omega)\\) in \\(V\\simeq a\\theta^2+b\\theta^4\\)."),
    sub("B.6. Potential plots"),
    p("Sketch V against angle for \\(\\omega/\\omega_c=0.9\\) and 5, without detailed plotting calculations."),
    sub("B.7. Critical-exponent analogy"),
    p("For magnetization \\(\\mathcal M=\\mathcal M_0(1-T/T_c)^{1/2}\\) below \\(T_c\\), identify mechanical analogues and the bead critical exponent."),
    sub("B.8. Small-oscillation frequency"),
    p("Find \\(\\Omega_0=R^{-1}\\sqrt{V^{\\prime\\prime}(\\theta_0)/m}\\) about stable equilibria."),
    sub("B.9. Frequency plot"),
    p("Sketch the small-oscillation frequency against rotation rate."),
    sub("C.1. Opposing-force equilibrium"),
    p("Now take \\(f=1,k=\\tan\\alpha\\). Write the limiting equilibrium condition as \\((\\omega/\\omega_c)^2=\\tan x/\\sin y\\), and identify x,y."),
    sub("C.2. Representative frictional angles"),
    p("For \\(f=1,k=0.05\\), estimate the equilibrium angles for \\(\\omega/\\omega_c=0.50\\) and 0.70."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("A Mechanical Model for Phase Transitions"),
    sub("A.1. Radial and tangential equations"),
    p("With N directed towards the centre, \\[mR\\dot\\theta^2=N-mg\\cos\\theta-mR\\omega^2\\sin^2\\theta,\\] \\[mR\\ddot\\theta=mR\\omega^2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta-mg\\sin\\theta-fkN.\\] These are the stipulated two-component model."),
    sub("B.1. Equilibrium branches"),
    p("The tangential equation gives \\(\\sin\\theta_0(\\omega^2\\cos\\theta_0-\\omega_c^2)=0\\). The branch \\(\\theta_0=0\\) always exists. For \\(\\omega>\\omega_c\\), additional branches are \\(\\theta_0=\\pm\\arccos(\\omega_c^2/\\omega^2)\\). The zero branch becomes unstable above threshold."),
    sub("B.2. Equilibrium-angle plot"),
    p("The stable zero branch continues to ratio 1, where it bifurcates continuously into two symmetric stable branches approaching \\(\\pm\\pi/2\\). Show the remaining zero branch as unstable above 1."),
    sub("B.3. Normal-reaction plot"),
    p("Below threshold the bead sits at the bottom and \\(N=mg\\). Above threshold substitute \\(\\cos\\theta_0=\\omega_c^2/\\omega^2\\) in the radial equation to get \\(N=mR\\omega^2\\). The curve is continuous at the threshold and then quadratic."),
    sub("B.4. Effective-potential coefficients"),
    p("Integrating the tangential force gives \\[V=mgR(1-\\cos\\theta)-\\tfrac12mR^2\\omega^2\\sin^2\\theta.\\] Thus \\(P=mgR,Q=-mgR,S=-mR^2\\omega^2/2\\)."),
    sub("B.5. Quartic expansion"),
    p("Expand both cosine and sine squared through fourth order: \\[a=\\frac{mgR}{2}(1-\\omega^2/\\omega_c^2),\\qquad b=\\frac{mgR}{6}(\\omega^2/\\omega_c^2-1/4).\\] In particular the quartic coefficient is positive near the transition."),
    sub("B.6. Potential plots"),
    p("At 0.9, V has one minimum at zero. At 5, the zero-angle point is a local maximum between symmetric minima at \\(\\pm\\arccos(1/25)\\). Use the full potential on the allowed interval for the distant minima, since a small-angle quartic expansion is not accurate there."),
    sub("B.7. Critical-exponent analogy"),
    p("Near threshold, \\(1-\\theta_0^2/2\\simeq\\omega_c^2/\\omega^2\\), so \\(\\theta_0\\simeq\\pm\\sqrt2(1-\\omega_c^2/\\omega^2)^{1/2}\\). Thus magnetization corresponds to \\(\\theta_0\\), reduced temperature to \\(\\omega_c^2/\\omega^2\\), and the exponent is \\(\\beta=1/2\\). Equivalently use \\(\\sin\\theta_0\\) and reduced temperature \\(\\omega_c^4/\\omega^4\\)."),
    sub("B.8. Small-oscillation frequency"),
    p("At zero below threshold, \\(\\Omega_0=\\sqrt{\\omega_c^2-\\omega^2}\\). At the nonzero stable branches above threshold, \\(\\Omega_0=\\omega\\sqrt{1-\\omega_c^4/\\omega^4}\\). Both tend to zero at threshold, where a harmonic approximation has zero stiffness."),
    sub("B.9. Frequency plot"),
    p("The curve starts at \\(\\Omega_0=\\omega_c\\) for zero rotation, decreases to zero at \\(\\omega_c\\), then rises and approaches \\(\\Omega_0\\sim\\omega\\). Both sides have the square-root softening near threshold."),
    sub("C.1. Opposing-force equilibrium"),
    p("With zero velocity and acceleration in the stipulated limiting-force model, \\[\\frac{\\omega^2}{\\omega_c^2}=\\frac{\\sin\\theta_0+k\\cos\\theta_0}{\\sin\\theta_0(\\cos\\theta_0-k\\sin\\theta_0)}=\\frac{\\tan(\\theta_0+\\alpha)}{\\sin\\theta_0}.\\] Thus \\(x=\\theta_0+\\alpha,y=\\theta_0\\). The equality prescribes a directional limiting friction force. True static friction would allow an interval bounded by this force rather than a unique equilibrium equality."),
    sub("C.2. Representative frictional angles"),
    p("Using \\(\\tan(\\theta_0+\\alpha)\\simeq\\theta_0+k\\) and \\(\\sin\\theta_0\\simeq\\theta_0\\), \\[\\theta_0\\simeq k/[(\\omega/\\omega_c)^2-1].\\] The estimates are \\(-0.0667\\) and \\(-0.0980\\) radians, rounded to \\(-0.07,-0.10\\) in the source. Exact roots of the limiting-force equation are approximately \\(-0.0665,-0.0971\\) radians."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2017-t3"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Space Debris"),
    p("A rocket stage is axisymmetric about body axis x, with principal inertias \\(J_x<J_y=J_z\\). Its centre of mass is C. Let angular momentum magnitude be L, making angle \\(\\theta\\) with x. Initially the tank is empty and external torques vanish. Residual fuel of negligible mass can later dissipate energy without external torque. The stage subsequently travels in a circular polar orbit of period \\(T=100\\,\\mathrm{min}\\). Its only conducting component is a nonmagnetic spherical tank shell, radius \\(R=4\\,\\mathrm m\\), thickness \\(D=2\\,\\mathrm{mm}\\), resistivity \\(\\rho=2.7\\times10^{-8}\\,\\Omega\\mathrm m\\). Ignore shell self-inductance. In the orbital XY plane, \\(\\mathbf r=R_O(\\cos u,\\sin u,0)\\), Earth magnetic dipole points along negative Y, and the equatorial field magnitude is \\(B_0=20\\,\\mu\\mathrm T\\). Use \\(\\mathbf B=\\mu_0[3(\\boldsymbol\\mu\\cdot\\mathbf r)\\mathbf r/r^5-\\boldsymbol\\mu/r^3]/(4\\pi)\\)."),
    fig("batch9-rocket-stage", "Original stage symmetry axis, centre of mass and spherical tank."),
    sub("A.1. Angular-velocity components"),
    fig("batch9-rocket-spin", "Original angular momentum and angular velocity relative to the body axis."),
    p("Find \\(\\omega_x,\\omega_y\\) in terms of L, angle and inertias."),
    sub("A.2. Rotational energy"),
    p("Find \\(E_x,E_y\\) and total E as a function of L and \\(\\cos\\theta\\)."),
    sub("A.3. Maximum axis excursion"),
    p("With initial angle \\(\\theta_0\\), find the largest angle between the symmetry axis and its initial direction."),
    sub("A.4. Precessing reference frame"),
    fig("batch9-rocket-precession", "Original precessing frame with y1 along fixed angular momentum."),
    p("A frame has y1 along fixed L and rotates so that x always lies in its x1y1 plane. Find its rate \\(\\Omega(t)\\), and the stage relative spin magnitude and angle to x."),
    sub("B.1. Dissipative end state"),
    p("For residual fuel sloshing with no external torque and initial \\(0<\\theta_1<\\pi/2\\), find the final angle \\(\\theta_2\\)."),
    sub("B.2. Final angular speed"),
    p("Initially \\(\\omega_1=1\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}\\), at \\(\\gamma_1=30^\\circ\\) to x. Use \\(J_x=4200,J_y=15000\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2}\\). Find the final speed."),
    sub("C.1. Induced magnetic moment"),
    fig("batch9-eddy-shell", "Original spherical shell, magnetic field and field-derivative axes."),
    p("For a uniform field with constant derivative \\(\\dot{\\mathbf B}\\) directed along y, find the shell magnetic-moment components."),
    sub("C.2. Shell torque"),
    p("The field is at angle \\(\\alpha\\) to its derivative as in the diagram. Find torque components."),
    sub("C.3. Orbital magnetic field"),
    fig("batch9-rocket-orbit", "Original polar orbital plane, Earth dipole and orbital-angle convention."),
    p("Find all inertial field components along the orbit."),
    sub("C.4. Torque on the rotating stage"),
    p("The stage rotates at signed angular speed \\(\\omega\\) along Z. Find torque versus u."),
    sub("C.5. Slow spin evolution"),
    p("Assume spin changes negligibly during one orbit. Find \\(\\omega(t)\\) from initial \\(\\omega_2\\)."),
    sub("C.6. Steady period ratio"),
    p("Find orbital period divided by stage rotation period in steady state."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Space Debris"),
    sub("A.1. Angular-velocity components"),
    p("Project angular momentum: \\(\\omega_x=L\\cos\\theta/J_x\\), \\(\\omega_y=L\\sin\\theta/J_y\\)."),
    sub("A.2. Rotational energy"),
    p("\\(E_x=L^2\\cos^2\\theta/(2J_x)\\), \\(E_y=L^2\\sin^2\\theta/(2J_y)\\). Hence \\[E=\\frac{L^2}{2J_y}+\\frac{L^2}{2}(J_x^{-1}-J_y^{-1})\\cos^2\\theta.\\]"),
    sub("A.3. Maximum axis excursion"),
    p("Energy and L conservation keep \\(\\cos^2\\theta\\), hence the continuous branch of \\(\\theta\\), fixed. The axis precesses on a cone about L. For the source acute-angle convention the maximum is \\(\\psi=2\\theta_0\\). For a directed axis with an obtuse initial angle the corresponding expression is \\(2\\min(\\theta_0,\\pi-\\theta_0)\\)."),
    sub("A.4. Precessing reference frame"),
    p("The relative spin must lie along x. Resolving \\(\\boldsymbol\\omega=\\boldsymbol\\Omega+\\boldsymbol\\omega_s\\), \\(\\Omega\\sin\\theta=L\\sin\\theta/J_y\\). Thus \\[\\Omega=L/J_y,\\quad\\omega_s=L\\cos\\theta_0(1/J_x-1/J_y),\\quad\\gamma_s=0\\] for the acute-angle convention. Both rates are constant."),
    sub("B.1. Dissipative end state"),
    p("L is fixed, and the coefficient of \\(\\cos^2\\theta\\) in E is positive. Its minimum is at \\(\\cos\\theta_2=0\\), hence \\(\\theta_2=\\pi/2\\). The stage rotates about a transverse maximum-inertia axis."),
    sub("B.2. Final angular speed"),
    p("\\(L=\\omega_1\\sqrt{J_x^2\\cos^2\\gamma_1+J_y^2\\sin^2\\gamma_1}\\) stays fixed. The final speed is \\(L/J_y=0.556\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}\\)."),
    sub("C.1. Induced magnetic moment"),
    p("Slice the shell into rings normal to the derivative. Faraday emf is \\(\\pi R^2\\sin^2\\phi\\,\\dot B\\), resistance \\(2\\pi\\rho\\sin\\phi/(D\\,d\\phi)\\), and moment element \\(-\\pi DR^4\\dot B\\sin^3\\phi\\,d\\phi/(2\\rho)\\). Since \\(\\int_0^\\pi\\sin^3\\phi\\,d\\phi=4/3\\), \\[\\boldsymbol\\mu=-K\\dot{\\mathbf B},\\qquad K=2\\pi DR^4/(3\\rho).\\] Thus \\((\\mu_x,\\mu_y,\\mu_z)=(0,-K\\dot B,0)\\)."),
    sub("C.2. Shell torque"),
    p("Torque is \\(\\boldsymbol\\mu\\times\\mathbf B\\). In the source axes it has \\(M_x=M_y=0\\), \\(M_z=K B\\dot B\\sin\\alpha\\). Its invariant vector form is \\(K\\mathbf B\\times\\dot{\\mathbf B}\\)."),
    sub("C.3. Orbital magnetic field"),
    p("Substitution in the dipole field gives \\[B_X=-\\tfrac32B_0\\sin2u,\\quad B_Y=\\tfrac12B_0(3\\cos2u-1),\\quad B_Z=0.\\] At u=0 this agrees with the given equatorial field."),
    sub("C.4. Torque on the rotating stage"),
    p("The derivative relevant to shell currents is the material derivative \\(\\mathbf q=\\dot{\\mathbf B}_E-\\boldsymbol\\omega\\times\\mathbf B_E\\), not just the orbital derivative. Use \\(\\mathbf M=K\\mathbf B_E\\times\\mathbf q\\). Then \\[M_X=M_Y=0,\\quad M_Z=K B_0^2\\left[\\frac{3\\pi}{T}(3-\\cos2u)-\\frac\\omega2(5-3\\cos2u)\\right].\\]"),
    sub("C.5. Slow spin evolution"),
    p("Orbit-average the cosine to zero: \\(J_y\\dot\\omega=KB_0^2(9\\pi/T-5\\omega/2)\\). Thus \\[\\omega(t)=\\frac{18\\pi}{5T}+\\left(\\omega_2-\\frac{18\\pi}{5T}\\right)e^{-\\delta t},\\quad\\delta=\\frac{5\\pi DB_0^2R^4}{3J_y\\rho}.\\] For the initial positive spin this remains positive and equals its magnitude."),
    sub("C.6. Steady period ratio"),
    p("The equilibrium rate is \\(18\\pi/(5T)\\). Hence \\(T/T_s=T\\omega_\\infty/(2\\pi)=9/5=1.8\\). The source final paragraph has a denominator typo in the angular-rate sentence. Its preceding evolution formula and final period ratio agree."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2016-t1"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Mechanics of a Deformable Lattice"),
    p("An N by N lattice, \\(N>1\\), contains \\(N^2\\) identical uniform equilateral triangular plates of side l and mass M. Equal-length massless rods connect freely hinged vertices. Four tubes confine the N edge vertices to slide along rails. The tubes form a diamond with fixed 60 and 120 degree corners. The top tube is horizontal, with top-left vertex A(0,0) fixed. The lattice has one degree of freedom \\(0\\le\\alpha\\le60^\\circ\\). Triangles are indexed \\((m,n)\\), \\(0\\le m,n<N\\), with vertices A,B,C in the diagrams. Motion lies in the xy plane, y positive upward, and potential zero at y=0. A plate has central inertia \\(Ml^2/12\\). Use gravitational acceleration g."),
    fig("batch9-lattice-configurations", "Original N by N hinged-triangle lattice at several deformation angles."),
    fig("batch9-lattice-indices", "Original triangle indices and vertices A B C with fixed top-left point and x-y axes."),
    sub("A.1. Four-plate potential"),
    fig("batch9-lattice-two", "Original four-plate lattice at four deformation angles."),
    p("For N=2, find total potential energy as a function of angle."),
    sub("A.2. Four-plate equilibrium"),
    p("Find the equilibrium angle for N=2."),
    sub("A.3. Four-plate oscillation"),
    p("Find kinetic energy in terms of \\(\\Delta\\dot\\alpha\\) for small oscillations and the ordinary frequency."),
    sub("B.1. General lattice equilibrium"),
    p("Find the equilibrium angle for arbitrary N."),
    sub("B.2. Large-lattice scaling"),
    p("At equilibrium as \\(N\\to\\infty\\), find exponents in \\(\\Delta E_p\\propto N^{\\gamma_1}\\), \\(E_k\\propto N^{\\gamma_2}\\), and \\(f_{E}^{\\prime}\\propto N^{\\gamma_3}\\), for fixed small angle and angular-speed perturbations."),
    sub("C.1. Optimal force point"),
    fig("batch9-lattice-force", "Original deformed lattice at 60 degrees and force-direction convention."),
    p("Choose one of the \\(3N^2\\) vertices where a force can maintain \\(\\alpha=60^\\circ\\) with minimum magnitude."),
    sub("C.2. Minimum holding force"),
    p("Find this force magnitude and its direction \\(\\theta_F\\) as shown in the diagram."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Mechanics of a Deformable Lattice"),
    sub("A.1. Four-plate potential"),
    p("The first plate centre is at \\(y_{00}=-l\\sin(\\pi/6+\\alpha)/\\sqrt3\\); each row is lower by \\(\\sqrt3l\\cos\\alpha\\). Thus \\[E_p=4Mg\\,y_{00}-2\\sqrt3Mgl\\cos\\alpha=-\\frac23Mgl(4\\sqrt3\\cos\\alpha+3\\sin\\alpha).\\]"),
    sub("A.2. Four-plate equilibrium"),
    p("Zero first derivative of the potential gives \\(4\\sqrt3\\sin\\alpha_E=3\\cos\\alpha_E\\), so \\(\\alpha_E=\\arctan(\\sqrt3/4)=0.40864\\,\\mathrm{rad}\\). The second derivative is positive."),
    sub("A.3. Four-plate oscillation"),
    p("Plate-centre coordinates are \\[x_{mn}=(2m+n)l\\cos\\alpha+\\frac l{\\sqrt3}\\cos(\\alpha+\\pi/6),\\quad y_{mn}=-\\sqrt3nl\\cos\\alpha-\\frac l{\\sqrt3}\\sin(\\alpha+\\pi/6).\\] Differentiate and sum \\(M(\\dot x^2+\\dot y^2)/2\\) over four plates. This gives \\(164Ml^2(\\Delta\\dot\\alpha)^2/57\\). Plate spin adds \\(Ml^2(\\Delta\\dot\\alpha)^2/6\\). Therefore \\[E_k=\\frac{347}{114}Ml^2(\\Delta\\dot\\alpha)^2.\\] Also \\(\\Delta E_p=\\sqrt{57}Mgl(\\Delta\\alpha)^2/3\\). Taking the ratio of the two quadratic coefficients yields \\[f_E=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\sqrt{\\frac{38\\sqrt{57}}{347}\\frac gl}.\\]"),
    sub("B.1. General lattice equilibrium"),
    p("Summing plate heights gives \\[E_p=-\\frac{N^2Mgl}{6}\\left[\\sqrt3(3N-2)\\cos\\alpha+3\\sin\\alpha\\right].\\] Differentiate: \\[\\alpha_{E}^{\\prime}=\\arctan\\frac{\\sqrt3}{3N-2}.\\]"),
    sub("B.2. Large-lattice scaling"),
    p("The potential curvature grows as \\(N^3\\). Since \\(\\alpha_{E}^{\\prime}\\sim1/N\\), differentiated centre coordinates remain order l per plate even at indices of order N. Each plate contributes order one translational and rotational inertia, so total inertia grows as \\(N^2\\). Hence \\((\\gamma_1,\\gamma_2,\\gamma_3)=(3,2,1/2)\\). The small-angle limit is taken locally about each equilibrium before comparing N scaling."),
    sub("C.1. Optimal force point"),
    p("Virtual work requires \\(F|\\partial\\mathbf r/\\partial\\alpha|\\ge|\\partial E_p/\\partial\\alpha|\\). The largest vertex displacement per angle is at C(N-1,N-1), so choose that vertex."),
    sub("C.2. Minimum holding force"),
    p("At \\(\\alpha=\\pi/3\\), \\(dE_p/d\\alpha=3(N-1)N^2Mgl/4\\). The C(N-1,N-1) derivative is \\((3N-2)l(-\\sqrt3/2,1/2)\\). A force along that derivative supplies positive virtual work, giving \\[F_{min}=\\frac{3(N-1)N^2}{4(3N-2)}Mg,\\qquad\\theta_F=5\\pi/6.\\] The force is 150 degrees from positive x, rather than necessarily perpendicular to the line from the fixed vertex."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2023-t2"] = {
  statement: [
    h("A Ball on a Turntable"),
    p("A ball of mass m, radius r and spherically symmetric mass distribution has inertia I about any central axis. It rolls without slipping on a horizontal turntable, assumed large enough that it never falls off. In the laboratory, its horizontal centre position is \\(\\mathbf R\\), velocity \\(\\mathbf v\\), and spin \\(\\boldsymbol\\omega\\). The turntable rotates about upward \\(\\hat{\\mathbf z}\\) at signed rate \\(\\Omega\\). Initial \\(\\mathbf R_0,\\mathbf v_0,\\Omega_0\\) are known, including vector directions. Write \\(J\\mathbf a=\\hat{\\mathbf z}\\times\\mathbf a\\), \\(\\alpha=I/(I+mr^2)\\), and \\(\\delta=I_d/(mr^2)\\), where \\(I_d\\) is turntable inertia. Part A fixes \\(\\Omega=\\Omega_0\\). Part B lets the table rotate freely. Part C adds uniformly distributed surface charge Q and vertical uniform field \\(\\mathbf B=B\\hat{\\mathbf z}\\), with B positive. Use the standard cross product. The source preamble has a typo in its z component."),
    fig("batch9-rolling-ball", "Original ball rolling on a turntable with centre distance R."),
    sub("A.1. Rolling constraint"),
    p("Express the laboratory ball velocity from the no-slip constraint."),
    sub("A.2. Ball acceleration"),
    p("Use force and torque equations to find the centre acceleration."),
    sub("A.3. Velocity-position relation"),
    p("Integrate the acceleration relation to express velocity using initial data and current position."),
    sub("A.4. Explicit trajectory"),
    p("Give the full trajectory for the prescribed initial data."),
    sub("A.5. Return to a table mark"),
    p("For a uniform solid ball, \\(I=2mr^2/5\\), choose circle radius \\(R_t=R_0\\). When does it return closest to the table mark initially beneath it?"),
    sub("B.1. Freely rotating constraint"),
    p("Find velocity and acceleration when the table rate varies."),
    sub("B.2. Table angular acceleration"),
    p("Find signed angular acceleration in terms of the current position and initial data."),
    sub("B.3. Table speed versus radius"),
    p("Find the table speed as a function of R alone."),
    sub("B.4. Maximum possible table speed"),
    p("Find the maximum possible speed at fixed initial radius and rate."),
    sub("B.5. Conic trajectories"),
    p("Find the conserved vertical angular momentum, remove constant ball spin, and derive the trajectory equation in coordinates with x along c. List the possible trajectory types."),
    sub("C.1. Charged-ball equations"),
    fig("batch9-charged-ball", "Original charged ball on the turntable in a vertical magnetic field."),
    p("Take \\(I=mr^2/10\\). Find the Newton and spin equations and the electromagnetic torque due to surface-charge rotation."),
    sub("C.2. Magnetic acceleration"),
    p("Eliminate friction and spin to find the ball acceleration for constant table rate."),
    sub("C.3. Removing the gyroscopic term"),
    p("Write \\(\\ddot{\\mathbf R}-\\gamma\\dot{\\mathbf R}\\times\\hat{\\mathbf z}+\\beta\\mathbf R=0\\). Find beta and gamma. Transform with \\(x=\\rho\\cos\\eta,y=\\rho\\sin\\eta\\) to remove first derivatives and classify the resulting radial equation."),
    sub("C.4. Expanding spiral"),
    p("Use \\(x(0)=1,y(0)=0,\\dot x(0)=1,\\dot y(0)=-1\\) in the linearly growing case \\(\\beta_{\\mathrm{prime}}=0\\). Find beta, gamma, table rates, charge sign and sketch the trajectory. The source leaves the condition beta prime equals zero implicit in this part."),
    sub("C.5. Spiral energy growth"),
    p("For the C.4 spiral, find leading total energy change after N much greater than one revolutions and the change per revolution. In this part use \\(m=r=1\\) and the explicit local value \\(I=1/11\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2}\\). Explain any inconsistency with the preceding inertia."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("A Ball on a Turntable"),
    sub("A.1. Rolling constraint"),
    p("The bottom contact velocity equals the local table velocity: \\(\\mathbf v-r\\boldsymbol\\omega\\times\\hat{\\mathbf z}=\\Omega\\hat{\\mathbf z}\\times\\mathbf R\\). Thus \\[\\mathbf v=\\Omega J\\mathbf R+r\\boldsymbol\\omega\\times\\hat{\\mathbf z}.\\]"),
    sub("A.2. Ball acceleration"),
    p("If friction is \\(\\mathbf F\\), then \\(m\\dot{\\mathbf v}=\\mathbf F\\) and \\(I\\dot{\\boldsymbol\\omega}=-rJ\\mathbf F\\). Differentiate the rolling constraint and eliminate friction to get \\(\\dot{\\mathbf v}=\\alpha\\Omega J\\mathbf v\\)."),
    sub("A.3. Velocity-position relation"),
    p("Constant \\(\\alpha\\Omega\\) allows direct integration: \\[\\mathbf v=\\mathbf v_0+\\alpha\\Omega J(\\mathbf R-\\mathbf R_0).\\]"),
    sub("A.4. Explicit trajectory"),
    p("Put \\(q=\\alpha\\Omega\\). Then \\[\\mathbf R(t)=\\mathbf R_0+\\frac{\\sin qt}{q}\\mathbf v_0+\\frac{1-\\cos qt}{q}J\\mathbf v_0.\\] It is a circle of radius \\(|\\mathbf v_0/q|\\), centred at \\(\\mathbf R_c=\\mathbf R_0+J\\mathbf v_0/q\\). The q=0 limit is straight-line motion."),
    sub("A.5. Return to a table mark"),
    p("The ball-circle angular rate is \\(q=2\\Omega/7\\). Both the ball and the mark certainly return together at \\(t=14\\pi/|\\Omega|\\). An earlier coincidence depends on the specified initial velocity direction and the second intersection of the two equal-radius circles. The source gives \\(14\\pi/(9|\\Omega|)\\) for a special crossing configuration, not a universal first-return time. To determine the actual earliest time for the given vectors solve \\(\\mathbf R(t)=\\mathcal R(\\Omega t)\\mathbf R_0\\) with the explicit A.4 trajectory. A tangent, coincident-circle or differently oriented initial geometry can change the earlier solutions."),
    sub("B.1. Freely rotating constraint"),
    p("Combining the rolling and spin equations still gives \\[\\dot{\\mathbf v}=\\alpha(\\dot\\Omega J\\mathbf R+\\Omega J\\mathbf v).\\] Integrating, \\(\\mathbf v=\\alpha\\Omega J\\mathbf R+\\mathbf c\\), where \\(\\mathbf c=\\mathbf v_0-\\alpha\\Omega_0J\\mathbf R_0\\) is constant."),
    sub("B.2. Table angular acceleration"),
    p("Table torque is \\(I_d\\dot\\Omega=-m(\\mathbf R\\times\\dot{\\mathbf v})_z\\). Substitution yields \\[(I_d+\\alpha mR^2)\\dot\\Omega=-\\alpha m\\Omega\\,\\mathbf c\\cdot\\mathbf R.\\] This determines the sign as well as the magnitude."),
    sub("B.3. Table speed versus radius"),
    p("Since \\(\\mathbf c\\cdot\\mathbf R=\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf R=\\tfrac12dR^2/dt\\), integration gives \\[\\Omega^2=\\Omega_0^2\\frac{I_d+\\alpha mR_0^2}{I_d+\\alpha mR^2}.\\] The sign of the rotation remains that of the initial rate when it is nonzero."),
    sub("B.4. Maximum possible table speed"),
    p("The denominator is smallest at R=0, giving \\[|\\Omega|_{max}=|\\Omega_0|\\sqrt{1+\\alpha mR_0^2/I_d}.\\] This is an upper bound. A particular trajectory need not pass through the centre."),
    sub("B.5. Conic trajectories"),
    p("Total vertical angular momentum is \\(M_z=I_d\\Omega+m(\\mathbf R\\times\\mathbf v)_z+I\\omega_z\\). The last term is constant, so define \\(L=M_z-I\\omega_z\\). With \\(\\mathbf c=c\\hat{\\mathbf x}\\), \\[L=\\Omega(I_d+\\alpha mR^2)-mcy.\\] Put \\(C=\\Omega_0^2(I_d+\\alpha mR_0^2)\\). Combining with B.3 gives \\[(L+mcy)^2=C[I_d+\\alpha m(x^2+y^2)].\\] This is a conic: ellipse if \\(C\\alpha m>m^2c^2\\), parabola if equal, hyperbola if smaller, with degenerate cases separately possible. Squaring loses the sign constraint \\(L+mcy=\\Omega(I_d+\\alpha mR^2)\\), so retain the branch consistent with the initial data. Several signs and coefficient expansions in the source solution are inconsistent. The conserved-form equation avoids them."),
    sub("C.1. Charged-ball equations"),
    p("The uniform surface charge has magnetic moment \\(\\boldsymbol\\mu=Qr^2\\boldsymbol\\omega/3\\). Thus \\[m\\dot{\\mathbf v}=\\mathbf F+Q\\mathbf v\\times\\mathbf B,\\quad I\\dot{\\boldsymbol\\omega}=-rJ\\mathbf F+\\frac{Qr^2}{3}\\boldsymbol\\omega\\times\\mathbf B.\\]"),
    sub("C.2. Magnetic acceleration"),
    p("Differentiate the rolling relation and substitute the C.1 equations. With \\(A=I+mr^2\\), \\[A\\ddot{\\mathbf R}=\\left(\\frac{4Qr^2B}{3}-I\\Omega\\right)\\dot{\\mathbf R}\\times\\hat{\\mathbf z}-\\frac{Qr^2B\\Omega}{3}\\mathbf R.\\] Every coefficient retains the signed table rate and charge."),
    sub("C.3. Removing the gyroscopic term"),
    p("\\[\\gamma=\\frac{4Qr^2B/3-I\\Omega}{I+mr^2},\\qquad\\beta=\\frac{Qr^2B\\Omega}{3(I+mr^2)}.\\] The numerator of beta includes Omega, omitted in one source line. A rotating-frame substitution with \\(\\eta=-\\gamma t/2+\\phi\\) gives \\(\\ddot\\rho+\\beta_{\\mathrm{prime}}\\rho=0\\), \\(\\beta_{\\mathrm{prime}}=\\beta+\\gamma^2/4\\), for solutions with a fixed direction in that rotating frame. More generally the two rotating Cartesian components each satisfy that oscillator equation. Positive beta prime gives harmonic components, zero gives linear components, negative gives exponential components. A general polar radius also has an angular-momentum term, so the scalar radial form is not universal."),
    sub("C.4. Expanding spiral"),
    p("The initial phase rate is -1, so \\(\\gamma=2\\), while \\(\\beta=-1\\). The solution is \\[x=(1+t)\\cos t,\\qquad y=-(1+t)\\sin t.\\] It spirals clockwise with linearly increasing radius. For \\(I=mr^2/10\\), \\(\\alpha=1/11\\) and \\(\\gamma=4\\beta/\\Omega-\\alpha\\Omega\\), so \\(\\Omega=-11\\pm\\sqrt{77}\\). Both table rates are negative. Since beta is proportional to Q times Omega and B is positive, Q is positive. The source says negative, contradicting its own beta formula. Initial data alone do not determine beta and gamma without the specified linearly growing case."),
    sub("C.5. Spiral energy growth"),
    p("For \\(\\rho=1+t\\), \\(v^2=1+\\rho^2\\). The horizontal spin from rolling is \\(\\boldsymbol\\omega_h=J\\mathbf v+\\Omega\\mathbf R\\), so \\(\\omega_h^2=1+(1+\\Omega)^2\\rho^2\\). Vertical spin is constant. Thus \\[\\Delta E=\\tfrac12[1+I(1+\\Omega)^2][(1+t)^2-1].\\] At \\(t\\simeq2\\pi N\\), \\(\\Delta E\\simeq2\\pi^2N^2[1+(1+\\Omega)^2/11]\\), with per-revolution increment \\(\\simeq4\\pi^2N[1+(1+\\Omega)^2/11]\\). The source replaces the signed factor by \\((1+|\\Omega|)^2\\), which is inconsistent with the rolling constraint for the clockwise spiral. Its explicit inertia 1/11 also differs from the earlier 1/10 mass distribution. Treat this part as a local changed-inertia evaluation. If recomputing C.4 for I=1/11, use \\(\\alpha=1/12\\) and \\(\\Omega=-12\\pm4\\sqrt6\\)."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2023-t3"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Cavitation"),
    p("A spherical bubble in infinite, homogeneous, inviscid, incompressible water contains vapour and possibly air. Neglect gravity, translation and air migration except where a question explicitly includes them. Vapour pressure remains at \\(p_v\\) through rapid evaporation and condensation, and water temperature is constant. Bubble radius is R, far-field pressure \\(p_\\infty\\), water density \\(\\rho=997\\,\\mathrm{kg/m^3}\\), surface tension \\(\\sigma=72.8\\times10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{N/m}\\), and \\(p_v=2340\\,\\mathrm{Pa}\\). Unless stated otherwise \\(p_\\infty=101\\,\\mathrm{kPa}\\). Air is ideal with adiabatic exponent \\(\\gamma=1.4\\), reference partial pressure q0 at radius R0, and atmospheric density \\(\\delta=1.29\\,\\mathrm{kg/m^3}\\). For dissolved air, Henry law is \\(u=Hq\\), \\(H=0.24\\times10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm{s^2/m^2}\\). Diffusivity is \\(\\kappa=2\\times10^{-9}\\,\\mathrm{m^2/s}\\), with outward flux \\(-\\kappa\\partial u/\\partial r\\). The supplied half-line heat-equation kernel is \\([e^{-(x-y)^2/(4t)}-e^{-(x+y)^2/(4t)}]/\\sqrt{4\\pi t}\\). Useful integrals are \\(\\int_0^\\infty e^{-bx^2}dx=\\sqrt\\pi/(2\\sqrt b)\\) and \\(\\int_0^\\infty x^2e^{-bx^2}dx=\\sqrt\\pi/(4b^{3/2})\\). Several numerical values in the retained solution use different constants from the statement. Use the statement constants here."),
    fig("batch9-cavitation-balance", "Original pressure-temperature phase diagram and spherical air-vapour bubble pressure balance."),
    sub("A.1. Collapse time scale"),
    p("Estimate the collapse time of a pure-vapour bubble with R0=1 mm, no surface tension, and the stated pressures. Set the dimensionless factor to one."),
    sub("A.2. Critical pressure"),
    p("A bubble with \\(R_0=10^{-5}\\,\\mathrm m\\) starts in equilibrium at 101 kPa. Find q0, then lower pressure gradually with isothermal air. Find the critical external pressure for unbounded growth."),
    sub("B.1. Bubble equation of motion"),
    p("Derive the radius equation and insert adiabatic air pressure."),
    sub("B.2. Exposure to vacuum"),
    p("A 10 micrometre nucleus initially in equilibrium at 101 kPa is suddenly exposed to vacuum. Estimate its asymptotic growth speed and the time scale to reach that regime."),
    sub("B.3. Pressure-jump rebound"),
    p("A 10 micrometre gas bubble starts in equilibrium at \\(p^-_\\infty=1600\\,\\mathrm{Pa}\\), then the far pressure jumps to 101 kPa. Estimate minimum radius before rebound."),
    sub("B.4. Final-collapse exponent"),
    p("For pure vapour with complete collapse at time T, find alpha in \\(R\\sim(T-t)^\\alpha\\)."),
    sub("B.5. Bubble resonance"),
    p("Find the natural radial frequency of an equilibrium bubble of radius R0=0.1 mm."),
    sub("B.6. Standing-wave force"),
    p("A small bubble at x=0 is driven by \\(p=p_0+A\\sin[k(x+a)]\\sin\\omega t\\), \\(\\omega=2\\pi f,k=\\omega/c\\). Find the mean force, treating the small response linearly away from resonance."),
    sub("C.1. Diffusive dissolution"),
    p("A nucleus with R0=10 micrometres and air pressure \\(q=1.70\\times10^5\\,\\mathrm{Pa}\\) lies in water saturated with air at atmospheric pressure. Neglect vapour. Estimate dissolution time, assuming diffusion extends much farther than the bubble radius."),
    sub("C.2. Air trapped in a crevice"),
    fig("batch9-cavitation-crevice", "Original conical air pocket, aperture alpha and contact angle theta."),
    p("A conical wall crevice has aperture alpha and water contact angle theta as shown. State mechanical and diffusive equilibrium and when the pocket survives."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Cavitation"),
    sub("A.1. Collapse time scale"),
    p("Dimensional analysis using the positive driving pressure gives \\[T\\sim R_0\\sqrt{\\rho/(p_\\infty-p_v)}=1.01\\times10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm s.\\] The retained solution prints 0.1 s, a factor of 1000 too large."),
    sub("A.2. Critical pressure"),
    p("Laplace balance gives \\(q_0=p_\\infty-p_v+2\\sigma/R_0=113220\\,\\mathrm{Pa}\\). Isothermal conservation gives \\(q=q_0R_0^3/R^3\\), hence \\(p_\\infty(R)=p_v+q_0R_0^3/R^3-2\\sigma/R\\). Its minimum occurs at \\(R_c=\\sqrt{3q_0R_0^3/(2\\sigma)}=48.3\\,\\mu\\mathrm m\\). Therefore \\(p_c=p_v-4\\sigma/(3R_c)\\simeq331\\,\\mathrm{Pa}\\). The published q0 and threshold use different surface-tension data."),
    sub("B.1. Bubble equation of motion"),
    p("Incompressibility gives radial water speed \\(v(r,t)=R^2\\dot R/r^2\\). Integrate the radial Euler equation from R to infinity and add the Laplace jump. The Rayleigh-Plesset equation without viscosity is \\[\\rho(R\\ddot R+\\tfrac32\\dot R^2)=p_v+q_0(R_0/R)^{3\\gamma}-p_\\infty-2\\sigma/R.\\] The vapour term stays constant while the air term changes adiabatically."),
    sub("B.2. Exposure to vacuum"),
    p("At large R the surface and air terms vanish. Then \\(\\dot R_\\infty=\\sqrt{2p_v/(3\\rho)}=1.25\\,\\mathrm{m/s}\\). Initially \\(\\ddot R(0)=101000/(\\rho R_0)=1.01\\times10^7\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}\\). Dividing the limiting speed by the initial acceleration gives an initial acceleration time of \\(1.2\\times10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm s\\). This is only a crude onset estimate, as in the source. Exact terminal speed is approached asymptotically and no finite settling tolerance is specified."),
    sub("B.3. Pressure-jump rebound"),
    p("Initial balance gives \\(q_0=1600-2340+14560=13820\\,\\mathrm{Pa}\\). Multiply the motion equation by \\(R^2\\dot R\\) and integrate. At a turning point, with \\(x=R/R_0\\), \\[0=\\frac{p_\\infty-p_v}{3}(x^3-1)+\\frac\\sigma{R_0}(x^2-1)+\\frac{q_0}{3(\\gamma-1)}(x^{3-3\\gamma}-1).\\] Select the smaller positive root, excluding initial x=1. It gives a radius of about 3 micrometres. The source estimate 0.4 micrometres uses a much smaller q0 that is inconsistent with the statement. The source small-radius asymptotic formula is not reliable at the corrected moderate compression."),
    sub("B.4. Final-collapse exponent"),
    p("The dominant inward kinetic-energy balance gives \\(\\dot R\\sim-R^{-3/2}\\). Integrating yields \\(R^{5/2}\\propto T-t\\), so \\(\\alpha=2/5\\)."),
    sub("B.5. Bubble resonance"),
    p("Linearize with \\(R=R_0(1+y)\\): \\[\\ddot y+\\omega_0^2y=0,\\quad\\omega_0^2=\\frac{3\\gamma q_0-2\\sigma/R_0}{\\rho R_0^2},\\quad q_0=p_\\infty-p_v+2\\sigma/R_0.\\] Hence \\(f_0=\\omega_0/(2\\pi)\\simeq32.6\\,\\mathrm{kHz}\\)."),
    sub("B.6. Standing-wave force"),
    p("The external pressure drives \\(\\rho R_0^2(\\ddot y+\\omega_0^2y)=-A\\sin(ka)\\sin\\omega t\\). Thus \\(y=-A\\sin(ka)\\sin\\omega t/[\\rho R_0^2(\\omega_0^2-\\omega^2)]\\). Average \\(F_x=-\\langle V\\partial_xp\\rangle\\) with \\(V\\simeq4\\pi R_0^3(1+3y)/3\\): \\[\\langle F_x\\rangle=\\frac{\\pi R_0A^2k\\sin(2ka)}{\\rho(\\omega_0^2-\\omega^2)}=\\frac{fA^2R_0\\sin(4\\pi fa/c)}{2\\rho c(f_0^2-f^2)}.\\] Below resonance bubbles are driven towards pressure antinodes. The retained solution loses the minus sign in the pressure forcing and consequently reverses the force. At resonance the undamped steady linear formula fails."),
    sub("C.1. Diffusive dissolution"),
    p("The spherical diffusion equation is \\(u_t=\\kappa(u_{rr}+2u_r/r)\\). The quasi-steady profile gives outward air loss \\(dm/dt=-4\\pi\\kappa RH(q-p_\\infty)\\). Mechanical equilibrium requires \\(q=p_\\infty+2\\sigma/R\\). With \\(m=(4\\pi/3)\\delta(q/p_\\infty)R^3\\) and \\(\\epsilon=2\\sigma\\delta/p_\\infty\\), integration gives \\[T=\\frac{\\delta R_0^3+\\epsilon R_0^2}{6\\sigma\\kappa H}\\simeq7\\,\\mathrm s.\\] Direct integration of \\(\\delta R^2+(2/3)\\epsilon R\\) produces epsilon with coefficient one, not four as printed in the retained solution. The stated q=170 kPa is also not in mechanical equilibrium at R0 with the other given constants, which instead imply 115.56 kPa. Using a fixed prescribed q throughout gives a different approximate diffusion-only model. The self-consistent equilibrium estimate above explains this limitation rather than imposing a unique numerical key."),
    sub("C.2. Air trapped in a crevice"),
    p("The interface obeys \\(p_v+q=p_\\infty\\pm2\\sigma/R\\) with curvature sign chosen from the geometry, and \\(u=Hq\\). A curvature that opposes diffusive squeezing allows a stable trapped pocket. With the source contact-angle convention its geometric condition is \\(2\\theta>\\pi+\\alpha\\). The angle convention and curvature orientation are essential."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2023-t1"] = {
  statement: [
    h("ISS Orbital Decay Dynamics"),
    p("Model the ISS as a satellite of mass \\(M_S=4.5\\times10^5\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\), effective area \\(S=2.5\\times10^3\\,\\mathrm{m^2}\\), length 109 m and panel width 73 m. Its nearly circular orbit has inclination \\(\\theta=51.6^\\circ\\). Use \\(R_E=6.38\\times10^6\\,\\mathrm m\\), \\(M_E=5.97\\times10^{24}\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\), \\(G=6.67\\times10^{-11}\\), \\(g_0=9.81\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}\\), \\(R=8.31\\,\\mathrm{J/(mol\\,K)}\\), \\(N_A=6.022\\times10^{23}\\,\\mathrm{mol^{-1}}\\), air molar mass \\(\\mu=0.029\\,\\mathrm{kg/mol}\\), surface density \\(\\rho_0=1.29\\,\\mathrm{kg/m^3}\\), \\(B=5.0\\times10^{-5}\\,\\mathrm T\\), and \\(e=1.60\\times10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm C\\). Treat the thermosphere as isothermal at T=425 K. This is the problem model, not a realistic atmosphere over all heights. Write \\(z=h/R_E\\) and \\(h_0=RT/(\\mu g_0)\\). Use atomic oxygen molar mass 0.016 kg/mol and daytime ion number density \\(n_i=10^{12}\\,\\mathrm{m^{-3}}\\), negligible at night. The retained answer sheet instead often uses ten times this ion density."),
    sub("A.1. General barometric formula"),
    p("Derive pressure at height h when gravity and temperature vary with height."),
    sub("A.2. Constant-gravity scale height"),
    p("Find the standard isothermal pressure law and calculate h0."),
    sub("A.3. Gravity-corrected pressure"),
    p("Keep the leading gravitational correction for \\(h\\ll R_E\\). Find improved pressure."),
    sub("A.4. Correction ratio"),
    p("Find \\(p_{imp}/p_{sta}\\) and evaluate at h=400 km."),
    sub("A.5. Density and number density"),
    p("Find the improved mass density and neutral-molecule number density."),
    sub("B.1. Circular-orbit kinematics"),
    p("Find speed and period at altitude h."),
    sub("B.2. Orbital energy"),
    p("Find the total energy in the circular approximation."),
    sub("B.3. Energy-loss balance"),
    p("Write energy balance under a tangential drag magnitude F."),
    sub("B.4. Positive descent speed"),
    p("Define positive \\(u_h=-dh/dt\\) and express it in terms of F."),
    sub("B.5. Loss per orbit and lifetime"),
    p("Find descent H per revolution and a formal lifetime integral for a height-dependent drag. For constant F simplify the lifetime."),
    sub("C.1. Neutral-air drag"),
    p("Assume molecules at rest are accelerated to satellite speed on collision. Find drag, descent speed and loss per orbit."),
    sub("C.2. Air-drag lifetime"),
    p("Find the formal lifetime under air drag for \\(h_0\\ll h\\ll R_E\\), using the improved density."),
    sub("D.1. Atomic-ion drag"),
    p("Find oxygen-ion mass density and the 24-hour averaged drag, taking day and night as equal."),
    sub("D.2. Ion-induced descent"),
    p("Find positive descent speed and loss per orbit due to the averaged ion drag."),
    sub("E.1. Collected electric current"),
    p("Estimate the current if every ion-electron pair swept over area S completes a circuit through the structure."),
    sub("E.2. Magnetic braking force"),
    p("Approximate conducting length as \\(\\sqrt S\\). With the supplied angular factor \\(s=\\sin(\\pi/2-\\theta)=\\cos\\theta\\), estimate the braking force. Discuss orbit averaging."),
    sub("E.3. Magnetic descent"),
    p("Find descent speed and loss per orbit from this model force."),
    sub("F.1. Numerical descent table"),
    fig("batch9-iss-history", "Original ISS altitude history and reboosts."),
    fig("batch9-iss-recent-altitude", "Original ISS mean altitude versus time in 2022 and 2023."),
    p("At h=350,375,400,410 km, calculate neutral lifetime, neutral descent, ion descent, magnetic descent and total descent, in days and m/day. Use the stated density and specify whether magnetic drag is daytime or full-day averaged. These columns replace the separate answer-sheet Table 1."),
    sub("F.2. Per-orbit numerical table"),
    p("At the same four altitudes calculate \\(H_{air},H_i,H_{ind}\\) in metres per orbit. These columns replace answer-sheet Table 2."),
    sub("F.3. Relative importance"),
    p("Rank the mechanisms above 380 km and identify the leading model contributions to orbital decay."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("ISS Orbital Decay Dynamics"),
    sub("A.1. General barometric formula"),
    p("Hydrostatic balance and the gas law give \\(dp/dh=-\\rho g\\), \\(\\rho=\\mu p/(RT)\\). Integrate: \\[p(h)=p(0)\\exp\\left[-\\frac\\mu R\\int_0^h\\frac{g(s)}{T(s)}ds\\right].\\]"),
    sub("A.2. Constant-gravity scale height"),
    p("For constant g0, \\(p_{sta}=p_0e^{-h/h_0}\\), with \\(h_0=RT/(\\mu g_0)=12414\\,\\mathrm m\\)."),
    sub("A.3. Gravity-corrected pressure"),
    p("\\(g(h)=g_0(1+h/R_E)^{-2}\\simeq g_0(1-2h/R_E)\\). Its integral is \\(g_0h(1-h/R_E)\\), giving \\[p_{imp}=p_0\\exp[-h(1-h/R_E)/h_0].\\] The exact isothermal result is \\(p_0\\exp[-h/(h_0(1+h/R_E))]\\). Accuracy refers to the expansion of the gravity integral, not to a uniformly small relative error after exponentiation."),
    sub("A.4. Correction ratio"),
    p("The ratio is \\(\\exp[h^2/(h_0R_E)]\\), about 7.54 at 400 km."),
    sub("A.5. Density and number density"),
    p("At fixed molar mass and temperature, \\[\\rho_h=\\rho_0e^{-h(1-h/R_E)/h_0},\\qquad n_h=N_A\\rho_h/\\mu.\\]"),
    sub("B.1. Circular-orbit kinematics"),
    p("With r=RE+h, \\(v_h=\\sqrt{GM_E/r}\\) and \\(\\tau_h=2\\pi\\sqrt{r^3/(GM_E)}\\). Equivalently use \\(GM_E=g_0R_E^2\\), with the slight rounding difference in the supplied constants."),
    sub("B.2. Orbital energy"),
    p("Kinetic energy is \\(GM_EM_S/(2r)\\) and gravitational energy is \\(-GM_EM_S/r\\), so \\[E=-GM_EM_S/(2r).\\]"),
    sub("B.3. Energy-loss balance"),
    p("\\[\\frac{GM_EM_S}{2r^2}\\frac{dh}{dt}=-Fv_h.\\] The drag does negative work and altitude decreases. The source uses a positive descent variable in a place labelled dh, producing a sign ambiguity. Here h is altitude and its derivative is negative."),
    sub("B.4. Positive descent speed"),
    p("From energy balance, \\[u_h=\\frac{2Fr^{3/2}}{M_S\\sqrt{GM_E}}=\\frac{2F}{M_S}\\sqrt{\\frac{R_E}{g_0}}(1+z)^{3/2}.\\]"),
    sub("B.5. Loss per orbit and lifetime"),
    p("\\[H_h=u_h\\tau_h=\\frac{4\\pi Fr^3}{GM_EM_S},\\quad T=\\frac{M_S\\sqrt{GM_E}}2\\int_0^h\\frac{ds}{F(s)(R_E+s)^{3/2}}.\\] For constant F, \\(T=M_S\\sqrt{GM_E}[R_E^{-1/2}-(R_E+h)^{-1/2}]/F\\). Extending this circular model to the ground is only a formal extrapolation."),
    sub("C.1. Neutral-air drag"),
    p("The swept mass rate is \\(\\rho_hSv_h\\), so \\(F_{air}=\\rho_hSv_h^2\\). Substitution gives \\[u_{air}=\\frac{2\\rho_hS\\sqrt{GM_Er}}{M_S},\\qquad H_{air}=\\frac{4\\pi\\rho_hSr^2}{M_S}.\\] No empirical drag coefficient or factor one-half is inserted in this collision model."),
    sub("C.2. Air-drag lifetime"),
    p("The integral is \\[T_{air}=\\frac{M_S}{2\\rho_0S\\sqrt{GM_E}}\\int_0^h\\frac{\\exp[s(1-s/R_E)/h_0]}{\\sqrt{R_E+s}}ds.\\] Endpoint asymptotics give \\(T_{air}\\simeq h_0/[u_{air}(h)(1-2h/R_E)]\\). Retain the improved exponential. The printed source lifetime drops its h squared correction although its drag retains it, causing inconsistent lifetimes."),
    sub("D.1. Atomic-ion drag"),
    p("\\(\\rho_i=0.016n_i/N_A\\). The daytime collision drag is \\(\\rho_iSv_h^2\\), and the full-day average is \\(F_i=\\rho_iSv_h^2/2\\)."),
    sub("D.2. Ion-induced descent"),
    p("\\[u_i=\\frac{\\rho_iS\\sqrt{GM_Er}}{M_S},\\qquad H_i=\\frac{2\\pi\\rho_iSr^2}{M_S}.\\] These values are ten times smaller for the stated ion density than in tables computed with \\(10^{13}\\,\\mathrm{m^{-3}}\\)."),
    sub("E.1. Collected electric current"),
    p("Each pair transfers one elementary charge through the circuit, giving daytime \\(I_{ind}\\sim eSn_iv_h\\). Do not count each electron and ion as two independent circuit charges."),
    sub("E.2. Magnetic braking force"),
    p("The model gives \\(F_{ind}=BI_{ind}\\sqrt S\\,s=eBn_iS^{3/2}v_hs\\). With the requested substitution \\(s=\\cos51.6^\\circ\\simeq0.621\\). A more detailed orbital average may use a different angular factor, but must state it. The source uses s approximately one and prints inconsistent numerical averages. For equal ion-free nights, halve this daytime force for a full-day average."),
    sub("E.3. Magnetic descent"),
    p("Daytime values are \\[u_{ind}=\\frac{2eBn_iS^{3/2}sr}{M_S},\\qquad H_{ind}=\\frac{4\\pi eBn_iS^{3/2}sr^{5/2}}{M_S\\sqrt{GM_E}}.\\] Full-day averaging halves both under the stated day/night simplification."),
    sub("F.1. Numerical descent table"),
    p("Use \\(\\rho_h\\) from A.5 and the three u expressions, multiplying m/s by 86400 for m/day. Compute neutral lifetime by the improved-density integral in C.2 or its stated endpoint approximation. Full-day averaged ion descent is about 0.66 m/day throughout this range. Magnetic daytime descent with s=0.621 is about 1.6 m/day, and full-day averaged about 0.8 m/day. Neutral descent falls rapidly with height. The retained tables use different ion density, angular averaging and an inconsistent neutral lifetime approximation, so compare conventions explicitly. Using \\(GM_E=g_0R_E^2\\), the improved density and full-day magnetic averaging, the columns below are h in km, neutral lifetime in days, and neutral, ion, magnetic and total descent in m/day: \\[\\begin{array}{r|rrrrr}h&T_{air}&u_{air}&u_i&u_{ind}&u_{total}\\\\350 & 80.9 & 171.81 & 0.66 & 0.80 & 173.28\\\\375 & 485.2 & 28.88 & 0.66 & 0.81 & 30.35\\\\400 & 2865.9 & 4.93 & 0.66 & 0.81 & 6.41\\\\410 & 5806.4 & 2.44 & 0.66 & 0.81 & 3.92\\end{array}\\] The lifetimes use numerical integration, not the endpoint approximation."),
    sub("F.2. Per-orbit numerical table"),
    p("Multiply each descent speed by the orbital period in seconds. Neutral descent drops steeply with altitude. The full-day averaged ion loss is roughly 0.043 m/orbit and magnetic loss roughly 0.10 m/orbit in the daytime convention, half the latter with full-day averaging. Use the explicit formulas to retain the chosen angle and density. For full-day magnetic averaging, the columns below are h in km, followed by neutral, ion and magnetic losses in m/orbit: \\[\\begin{array}{r|rrr}h&H_{air}&H_i&H_{ind}\\\\350 & 10.917 & 0.042 & 0.051\\\\375 & 1.845 & 0.042 & 0.051\\\\400 & 0.317 & 0.043 & 0.052\\\\410 & 0.157 & 0.043 & 0.052\\end{array}\\] The source rounded table is not a unique key for the changed conventions."),
    sub("F.3. Relative importance"),
    p("The ranking depends on the supplied ion density and averaging choice as well as altitude. Neutral density decreases exponentially while the ion and magnetic model terms vary slowly. The source ranks magnetic, ion, neutral using its larger ion density and s approximately one. With the statement density and the requested angular factor, neutral drag can still dominate near 380 to 400 km. There is no altitude-independent ranking over all h greater than 380 km under these changed conventions. Compare the table rather than extrapolating one rounded ordering."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2015-t2"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Solar Wind and Aurora"),
    p("Study charged particles in the terrestrial magnetosphere. Use \\(c=2.998\\times10^8\\,\\mathrm{m/s}\\), \\(\\epsilon_0=8.9\\times10^{-12}\\), \\(\\mu_0=4\\pi\\times10^{-7}\\), \\(e=1.6\\times10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm C\\), electron mass \\(m=9.1\\times10^{-31}\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\), proton mass \\(m_p=1.67\\times10^{-27}\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\), \\(k=1.38\\times10^{-23}\\), \\(g=9.8\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}\\), \\(h=6.626\\times10^{-34}\\), \\(R_E=6.4\\times10^6\\,\\mathrm m\\), and equatorial surface dipole strength \\(B_0=3.1\\times10^{-5}\\,\\mathrm T\\). For a dipole directed down the z axis, use \\[\\mathbf B_d=\\frac{B_0R_E^3}{r^5}[-3xz\\hat{\\mathbf x}-3yz\\hat{\\mathbf y}+(x^2+y^2-2z^2)\\hat{\\mathbf z}],\\quad r\\ge R_E.\\] The original statement prints a plus sign on the x term, inconsistent with an axial dipole and the later solution. The corrected dipole convention is explicit here. Dayside reconnection permits some solar-wind particles to reach the magnetotail and subsequently precipitate into the thermosphere."),
    sub("a.i. Electron gyro-orbit"),
    fig("batch9-electron-gyro", "Original electron launched from the origin in the uniform magnetic field."),
    p("An electron starts at the origin with velocity \\(v\\hat{\\mathbf y}\\) perpendicular to uniform \\(\\mathbf B=B\\hat{\\mathbf z}\\). Find its trajectory."),
    sub("a.ii. Current and magnetic moment"),
    p("Find the current magnitude and the electron orbital magnetic-moment vector."),
    sub("a.iii. Helical pitch"),
    p("If the initial velocity makes angle \\(0<\\theta<90^\\circ\\) with B, find the helix pitch."),
    sub("b.i. Diamagnetic current"),
    p("In uniform B along z, ions and electrons have equal temperature and density \\(n(x)=n_0+\\alpha x\\), with \\(\\alpha>0\\). Explain the current generation with a sketch. Pressure is \\(p=2knT\\)."),
    sub("b.ii. Plasma magnetization"),
    p("For Maxwellian ions and electrons, find beta in the signed magnetization \\(M=\\beta n(x)kT/B\\). You may use \\(\\int_0^\\infty xe^{-x}dx=1\\) and \\(\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{-x^2}dx=\\sqrt\\pi\\)."),
    sub("c. Diamagnetic field ratio"),
    p("At \\((x,y,z)=(10R_E,0,R_E)\\), use \\(p(z)=p_0e^{-(z/a)^2}\\), \\(p_0=3\\times10^{-10}\\,\\mathrm{Pa}\\), \\(a=2R_E\\), and local \\(B_{dia}=\\mu_0M\\). Calculate the ratio of diamagnetic and dipole field magnitudes, noting the different field-axis orientation from part b."),
    sub("d.i. Adiabatic magnetic moment"),
    fig("batch9-magnetic-mirror", "Original field minimum P2 and maxima P1 P3 with converging field lines."),
    p("For a slowly varying magnetic mirror, \\(\\langle B_r\\rangle=-r_c(dB/ds)/2\\), with \\(|B_r|\\ll B_z\\). Derive the gyro-averaged parallel force and show that \\(\\mu=mv_\\perp^2/(2B)\\) is conserved."),
    sub("d.ii. Mirror confinement"),
    p("At the field minimum Bmin the pitch angle is theta. The mirror maximum is Bmax. Find the trapping condition."),
    sub("e. Auroral loss cone"),
    fig("batch9-terrestrial-pitch", "Original dipole field line and particle starting at the equatorial radius."),
    p("A particle starts at the equator at x=6RE with pitch theta. In the dipole approximation and ignoring drift, determine the pitch-angle condition for it to reach below 200 km near latitude 60 degrees."),
    sub("f.i. Atmospheric stratification"),
    p("Assume isothermal ideal gas with \\(\\rho_0g/P_0=0.13\\,\\mathrm{km^{-1}}\\). Find density versus altitude and the oxygen-density ratio at 160 and 220 km."),
    sub("f.ii. Auroral colours"),
    fig("batch9-oxygen-levels", "Original oxygen ground and two excited states with their energies and lifetimes."),
    p("Oxygen excited states lie 1.96 and 4.17 eV above ground, with lifetimes 110 s and 0.8 s. Collision frequency is \\(\\nu=10^9\\rho/\\rho_0\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}\\). Predict colours at 160 and 220 km."),
    sub("g. Solar-wind compression"),
    p("Find the minimum solar-wind speed pushing the dayside magnetopause to geosynchronous radius. The wind has field \\(B_s=5\\times10^{-9}\\,\\mathrm T\\) and density 50 protons per cubic centimetre. Ignore inner plasma pressure and changes of the dipole. Use the stated energy-density balance with magnetic pressure \\(B^2/(2\\mu_0)\\)."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Solar Wind and Aurora"),
    sub("a.i. Electron gyro-orbit"),
    p("Let \\(\\omega_c=eB/m>0\\), \\(r_c=mv/(eB)\\). Newton gives \\(\\dot v_x=-\\omega_cv_y,\\dot v_y=\\omega_cv_x\\). With the prescribed initial conditions, \\[x=r_c(\\cos\\omega_ct-1),\\quad y=r_c\\sin\\omega_ct,\\quad z=0.\\] It is a circle centred at \\((-r_c,0)\\), not at the initial position. The source unshifted circle misses the origin condition."),
    sub("a.ii. Current and magnetic moment"),
    p("\\(I=e/T=e^2B/(2\\pi m)\\). The conventional current circulates opposite to the electron, so \\[\\boldsymbol\\mu=-\\frac{mv_\\perp^2}{2B}\\hat{\\mathbf B}.\\] It is diamagnetic."),
    sub("a.iii. Helical pitch"),
    p("The parallel speed stays \\(v\\cos\\theta\\); multiply it by \\(2\\pi m/(eB)\\). The pitch is \\(2\\pi mv\\cos\\theta/(eB)\\), with orbit radius \\(mv\\sin\\theta/(eB)\\)."),
    sub("b.i. Diamagnetic current"),
    p("Opposite gyration senses and more guiding centres on the denser side produce charge currents in the same transverse direction. Magnetization is \\(\\mathbf M=-(p/B)\\hat{\\mathbf z}\\), so \\(\\mathbf j_M=\\nabla\\times\\mathbf M=(2kT\\alpha/B)\\hat{\\mathbf y}\\). Draw excess conventional current along positive y. This satisfies \\(\\mathbf j\\times\\mathbf B=\\nabla p\\)."),
    sub("b.ii. Plasma magnetization"),
    p("For either species, \\(\\langle mv_\\perp^2/2\\rangle=kT\\). Each therefore contributes \\(-nkT/B\\) along B. Together \\(M=-2nkT/B=-p/B\\), giving beta=-2."),
    sub("c. Diamagnetic field ratio"),
    p("Use \\(|\\mathbf B_d|^2=B_0^2(30^2+98^2)/101^5\\). Then \\(|B_{dia}|/|B_d|=\\mu_0p/|B_d|^2\\simeq0.306\\). The source value about one divides by the squared x component alone. That is not the total dipole-field magnitude. The local magnetization opposes the total local field, so use its full magnitude unless a projected-component ratio is explicitly requested."),
    sub("d.i. Adiabatic magnetic moment"),
    p("Gyro-averaging the axial Lorentz force gives \\(F_\\parallel=-\\mu\\,dB/ds\\), where mu is the positive moment magnitude. Then \\(d(mv_\\parallel^2/2)/dt=-\\mu\\,dB/dt\\). Total kinetic energy is constant, while perpendicular energy is \\(\\mu B\\). Its derivative must be \\(\\mu\\,dB/dt\\), so \\(B\\,d\\mu/dt=0\\). Thus mu is adiabatically conserved under the stated slow-field approximation."),
    sub("d.ii. Mirror confinement"),
    p("At reflection \\(v_\\parallel=0\\). Energy and mu conservation give \\(\\sin^2\\theta\\ge B_{min}/B_{max}\\). Thus \\[\\theta\\ge\\arcsin\\sqrt{B_{min}/B_{max}}.\\] The source final arcsine omits the square root, although its preceding velocity inequality requires it."),
    sub("e. Auroral loss cone"),
    p("The initial strength is \\(B_i=B_0/216\\). At \\(r=R_E+200\\,\\mathrm{km}\\), latitude 60 degrees, \\(B_L=B_0(R_E/r)^3\\sqrt{1+3\\sin^260^\\circ}\\). To avoid mirroring above that level, \\(\\sin^2\\theta<B_i/B_L\\), yielding roughly \\(\\theta<3.0^\\circ\\). Neglecting the 200 km altitude gives about 2.9 degrees. A single ideal dipole line from 6RE reaches the surface nearer 66 degrees, so the source specified latitude is an approximate target-field estimate rather than an exact field-line intersection."),
    sub("f.i. Atmospheric stratification"),
    p("With constant g over the height range, hydrostatic balance gives \\(\\rho(H)=\\rho_0e^{-0.13H}\\), H in km. Hence \\(\\rho(160)/\\rho(220)=e^{7.8}=2.44\\times10^3\\). Keeping inverse-square gravity instead gives \\(\\rho/\\rho_0=\\exp[-(\\rho_0gR_E/P_0)(1-R_E/(R_E+H))]\\)."),
    sub("f.ii. Auroral colours"),
    p("At 160 km, \\(\\nu\\simeq0.93\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}\\), quenching the long-lived first state while the short-lived second state can radiate to the first. The photon has energy 2.21 eV and wavelength about 562 nm, green. At 220 km, \\(\\nu\\simeq3.8\\times10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}\\), so the first state survives and can radiate to ground at about 633 nm, red. The source assumes the lower excited state is more populated to identify the dominant red light. Other lines can still occur."),
    sub("g. Solar-wind compression"),
    p("For a 24-hour period, \\(r_g^3=gR_E^2(86400/(2\\pi))^2\\), so \\(r_g\\simeq6.59R_E\\). Local dipole field is \\(B_m=B_0(R_E/r_g)^3\\). The model balance is \\(\\rho_sv^2/2+B_s^2/(2\\mu_0)=B_m^2/(2\\mu_0)\\), with \\(\\rho_s=50\\times10^6m_p\\). Hence \\[v=\\sqrt{(B_m^2-B_s^2)/(\\mu_0\\rho_s)}\\simeq330\\,\\mathrm{km/s}.\\] This uses the source energy-density convention. A fluid momentum-flux pressure convention would replace the one-half kinetic factor and give a different threshold."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2022-t2"] = {
  statement: [
    h("James Webb Space Telescope"),
    p("In the stated model a star illuminates a primary mirror of area \\(A_m=25\\,\\mathrm{m^2}\\), effective focal length \\(f=130\\,\\mathrm m\\), focusing onto a CCD. A red giant at distance 89 light-years has temperature 3600 K and diameter \\(d_o=1.7\\times10^{11}\\,\\mathrm m\\). Use \\(\\lambda=800\\,\\mathrm{nm}\\), \\(c=2.998\\times10^8\\,\\mathrm{m/s}\\), \\(k_B=1.380649\\times10^{-23}\\,\\mathrm{J/K}\\), \\(\\sigma=5.670374419\\times10^{-8}\\,\\mathrm{W/(m^2K^4)}\\), and 365 days per year. Dark current is \\(i_d=i_0e^{-|\\Delta E_g|/(6k_BT)}\\), measured as electrons per second. The detector-count graph is retained below. Part C uses five parallel, thin, square sunshield sheets, each 200 square metres, separated by 25 cm at the perimeter, with small emissivity epsilon on both faces and diffuse reflections. Net intersheet exchange is \\(\\alpha Q_{ij}\\), gap loss is \\(\\beta Q_{ij}\\), with \\(Q_{ij}=\\epsilon\\sigma A_s(T_i^4-T_j^4)\\), following the stipulated approximate difference-flux convention. Space background is negligible. Part D studies adiabatic steady helium throttling through a porous plug from states 1 to 2, with negligible change in bulk kinetic energy. Helium is not ideal and stays gaseous."),
    sub("A.1. Geometrical image size"),
    p("Calculate the focused geometrical image diameter."),
    sub("A.2. Diffraction spot"),
    p("Estimate the diameter of the central diffraction maximum."),
    sub("A.3. Uncooled detector temperature"),
    p("If the black detector can radiate only from its top imaging surface, find equilibrium temperature at the stellar image, with formula and estimate."),
    sub("B.1. Thermal threshold scale"),
    fig("batch9-dark-current", "Original logarithmic detector dark current versus inverse temperature with labelled axes."),
    p("Use the dark-current plot to estimate the temperature scale of thermal photons capable of exciting a detector electron."),
    sub("B.2. Count uncertainty"),
    p("For readout uncertainty \\(\\sigma_r\\), dark current id, photon rate p and exposure tau, find total uncertainty."),
    sub("B.3. Ten-sigma photon rate"),
    p("Use detector temperature 7.5 K, exposure \\(10^4\\,\\mathrm s\\) and read noise 14. Find minimum photon rate such that its count equals ten times total uncertainty."),
    sub("B.4. Incident photon intensity"),
    p("Take photon energy just equal to the inferred gap. Find irradiance on the primary mirror."),
    sub("C.1. Five-sheet temperatures"),
    fig("batch9-shield-flow", "Original five sunshield sheets with interlayer and escaping radiation."),
    p("Derive first- and fifth-sheet equilibrium temperatures in terms of incident solar intensity I0, alpha and beta."),
    sub("C.2. Geometrical exchange estimates"),
    fig("batch9-shield-gap", "Original two adjacent finite sheets and the open perimeter gap."),
    p("For epsilon=0.05, estimate alpha and beta using the gap as a black absorbing perimeter."),
    sub("C.3. Numerical shield temperatures"),
    p("Use \\(I_0=1360\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}\\) and your estimates to find T1 and T5."),
    sub("D.1. Throttling comparisons"),
    fig("batch9-porous-plug", "Original helium entering and leaving an insulating porous plug, states 1 and 2."),
    p("For one mole compare state 1 versus 2 for internal energy U, temperature T, entropy S, pressure P and volume V. Use greater, less, equal or undetermined. Both pipes have equal cross section and downstream bulk speed is slightly greater."),
    sub("D.2. Conserved throttling quantity"),
    p("Find the conserved combination of U,P,V and derive it."),
    sub("D.3. Helium enthalpy graph"),
    fig("batch9-helium-uv", "Original helium specific-internal-energy versus specific-volume graph, with isentropes and isotherms."),
    p("For specific volume \\(V_2=0.100\\,\\mathrm{m^3/kg}\\) and T2=7.5 K, use the original helium U-V graph to determine specific enthalpy. Solid curves are isentropes, dashed curves isotherms. Show the construction."),
    sub("D.4. Maximum inlet temperature"),
    p("Use the same graph to find the highest possible inlet temperature consistent with the conserved enthalpy."),
    sub("D.5. Inlet pressure"),
    p("For the maximum inlet temperature find P1 from the graph."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("James Webb Space Telescope"),
    sub("A.1. Geometrical image size"),
    p("The small angular size is \\(d_o/D\\). Thus \\(d_i=fd_o/D=26.2\\,\\mu\\mathrm m\\)."),
    sub("A.2. Diffraction spot"),
    p("Use an equal-area circular aperture \\(D_m=2\\sqrt{A_m/\\pi}=5.64\\,\\mathrm m\\). The Airy diameter is \\(d_d=2.44\\lambda f/D_m=45.0\\,\\mu\\mathrm m\\). The problem is an estimate, so omitting the Airy factor yields a somewhat smaller accepted scale."),
    sub("A.3. Uncooled detector temperature"),
    p("The collected power is \\(P=A_m\\sigma T_*^4(d_o/(2D))^2\\). If it is uniformly deposited over spot diameter d, balance \\(P=\\sigma T_i^4\\pi d^2/4\\): \\[T_i=T_*\\left(\\frac{A_md_o^2}{\\pi D^2d^2}\\right)^{1/4}.\\] With geometrical d=di this is about 530 K. Taking the source approximate broadened diameter d=di+dd gives about 320 K. A diffraction-only area gives about 410 K. State the spot model. A real point-spread function has nonuniform intensity, so these are effective spot temperatures."),
    sub("B.1. Thermal threshold scale"),
    p("The graph slope of log base 10 current against 1/T is approximately -100 K. Hence \\(|\\Delta E_g|/(6k_B)\\simeq100\\ln10=230\\,\\mathrm K\\), and the characteristic photon thermal-energy scale is \\(|\\Delta E_g|/k_B\\simeq1380\\,\\mathrm K\\), of order \\(10^3\\,\\mathrm K\\). A thermal spectrum has no sharp cutoff, so the intended answer is a scale."),
    sub("B.2. Count uncertainty"),
    p("Independent variances add: \\[\\sigma_t=\\sqrt{\\sigma_r^2+(i_d+p)\\tau}.\\]"),
    sub("B.3. Ten-sigma photon rate"),
    p("Read id approximately 5 electrons/s from the graph. Put P=p tau. Solve \\(P^2=100(14^2+5\\times10^4+P)\\), obtaining P approximately 2291 and \\(p=0.229\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}\\). Graph-reading uncertainty gives roughly 0.206 to 0.250 per second."),
    sub("B.4. Incident photon intensity"),
    p("The threshold photon energy is about \\(1380k_B=1.91\\times10^{-20}\\,\\mathrm J\\). Thus \\(I=p\\Delta E_g/A_m\\simeq1.75\\times10^{-22}\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}\\)."),
    sub("C.1. Five-sheet temperatures"),
    p("Let \\(q_i=\\epsilon\\sigma A_sT_i^4\\), \\(f_i=\\alpha(q_i-q_{i+1})\\), and \\(r=1+\\beta/\\alpha\\). The source recurrence is \\(f_{i-1}=rf_i\\), with \\(f_4=q_5\\). Summing differences gives \\(q_1=Kq_5\\), \\[K=1+(1+r+r^2+r^3)/\\alpha.\\] Sheet-one balance is \\(\\epsilon A_sI_0=q_1+rf_1=(K+r^4)q_5\\). Hence \\[T_5=\\left[\\frac{I_0}{\\sigma(K+r^4)}\\right]^{1/4},\\quad T_1=\\left[\\frac{I_0K}{\\sigma(K+r^4)}\\right]^{1/4}.\\] This explicitly follows the stipulated difference-flux recurrence. A physical view-factor model for gap radiation instead depends on a sum of surface emissions. The official discussion acknowledges that distinction, so do not combine the two models silently."),
    sub("C.2. Geometrical exchange estimates"),
    p("The gap area is \\(A_g=4h\\sqrt{A_s}=14.14\\,\\mathrm{m^2}\\). Repeated diffuse reflection weights the two sheet absorbing areas epsilon As and the open gap. Therefore \\(\\alpha\\simeq\\epsilon A_s/(2\\epsilon A_s+A_g)=0.293\\), while the escape probability is \\(\\beta_{\\mathrm{prime}}\\simeq A_g/(2\\epsilon A_s+A_g)=0.414\\). This is a probability for total radiation, not automatically the beta multiplying a temperature difference. Using beta approximately beta prime is the elementary problem approximation. A conversion requires temperature ratios and gives a somewhat larger effective beta."),
    sub("C.3. Numerical shield temperatures"),
    p("Using the explicit C.1 recurrence with alpha=0.293 and beta=0.414 gives r=2.414, K=80.60 and \\(T_1\\simeq360\\,\\mathrm K,T_5\\simeq120\\,\\mathrm K\\). Different self-consistent gap approximations change the estimates. The retained official solution tabulates roughly 380 K and 102 K for rounded alpha=0.3,beta=0.4, but this does not follow the displayed recurrence used here. Formula and convention consistency matter, so this is self-review."),
    sub("D.1. Throttling comparisons"),
    p("Pressure decreases, entropy increases through irreversibility, and equal-area mass continuity with higher downstream speed implies molar volume increases. Thus P1>P2, S1<S2, V1<V2. For an unspecified real gas the signs of T2-T1 and U2-U1 are not determined by these facts alone. The official key asserts U1<U2 from viscous dissipation, but this neglects the competing pressure-work terms. From conserved enthalpy, U2-U1=P1V1-P2V2, whose sign needs an equation of state."),
    sub("D.2. Conserved throttling quantity"),
    p("Upstream gas does work P1V1 on a mole entering the plug, while that mole does P2V2 on downstream gas. No heat is exchanged and kinetic energy changes are negligible, so \\(U_2-U_1=P_1V_1-P_2V_2\\). Thus \\(H=U+PV\\) is constant. More precisely steady flow conserves specific enthalpy plus bulk kinetic energy per mass."),
    sub("D.3. Helium enthalpy graph"),
    p("At the specified point draw the local isentrope tangent. Since \\((\\partial U/\\partial V)_S=-P\\), its intercept at V=0 is U+PV. Bracket with nearby tangents and interpolate: approximately \\(40.7\\,\\mathrm{kJ/kg}\\). The additional official answer-sheet graph and full solution are supplied because the archive retained solution ends before Part D."),
    sub("D.4. Maximum inlet temperature"),
    p("From the enthalpy intercept draw candidate tangents to isentropes. Their contact points trace states of the same enthalpy. The largest isotherm touched is approximately 11 K. Above that inlet temperature no state on the required enthalpy locus reaches the specified outlet."),
    sub("D.5. Inlet pressure"),
    p("Pressure is minus the isentrope tangent slope. With the constructed inlet point, the graph gives approximately \\(1.8\\,\\mathrm{MPa}\\), with rough graphical estimates around 1.6 to 2.4 MPa."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2025-t1"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Hydrogen and Galaxies"),
    p("Use a nonrelativistic Bohr hydrogen model with fixed proton, electron mass me and charge magnitude e. Orbital angular momentum is \\(L=n\\hbar\\), n a positive integer, and \\(\\alpha=e^2/(4\\pi\\epsilon_0\\hbar c)\\simeq7.27\\times10^{-3}\\). Use \\(\\hbar\\), not h, except for photon energy written as \\(2\\pi\\hbar c/\\lambda\\). For numerical work use \\(m_e=9.109\\times10^{-31}\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\), \\(m_p=1.673\\times10^{-27}\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\), \\(c=2.998\\times10^8\\,\\mathrm{m/s}\\), \\(\\hbar=1.055\\times10^{-34}\\,\\mathrm{J\\,s}\\), \\(e=1.602\\times10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm C\\), \\(G=6.67\\times10^{-11}\\), \\(1\\,\\mathrm{kpc}=3.09\\times10^{19}\\,\\mathrm m\\), and \\(M_\\odot=1.99\\times10^{30}\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\). Later parts study the supplied galaxy rotation, hydrogen radio and Tully-Fisher graphs. The stipulated spin moment is \\(\\mathcal M_s=e\\hbar/m_e\\), with \\(\\Delta E_{HF}\\simeq3.72(m_e/m_p)\\Delta E_F\\). This is the question model, not a precision atomic calculation."),
    sub("A.1. Circular electron speed"),
    p("Find speed v at orbital radius r."),
    sub("A.2. Bohr orbit scales"),
    p("Show \\(r_n=n^2r_1\\). Find r1 in terms of alpha, me,c,hbar and evaluate it to three digits. Express v1 in terms of alpha and c."),
    sub("A.3. Bound-state energy"),
    p("Find En in terms of e, epsilon0,r1,n and E1 in terms of alpha,me,c. Give E1 in eV."),
    sub("A.4. Proton-current field"),
    p("In the electron frame a proton orbits at radius r1 and speed v1. Find the field magnitude B1 at the electron."),
    sub("A.5. Hyperfine emission"),
    p("Find the parallel-antiparallel splitting Delta EF in terms of alpha and E1. Then find the hyperfine photon wavelength and evaluate it to two digits."),
    sub("B.1. Circular galactic motion"),
    p("For a spherically symmetric gravitational potential per mass phi(r), find circular speed in terms of r and dphi/dr."),
    sub("B.2. Visible-bulge mass"),
    fig("batch9-galaxy-rotation", "Original galaxy photograph and radial rotation measurements with the red bulge-only prediction."),
    p("The red curve assumes a homogeneous bulge within 1 kpc and Keplerian motion outside it. Estimate the NGC 6946 bulge mass from that curve, in solar masses."),
    sub("B.3. Extended density model"),
    p("Take \\(\\rho_m=C_m/(r_m^2+r^2)\\), Cm and rm positive. Show \\(v_{c,m}=\\sqrt{k_1-k_2\\arctan(r/r_m)/r}\\), find k1,k2, and give small- and large-radius limits. Simplify enclosed mass at large r and estimate mass within the visible image. You may use \\(\\int_0^r x^2/(a^2+x^2)dx=r-a\\arctan(r/a)\\)."),
    sub("C.1. Vertical galactic stability"),
    p("Let \\(\\varphi_G(r,z)=\\varphi_0\\ln(r/r_0)\\exp[-(z/z_0)^2]\\), with phi0 positive and r<r0. At constant r derive the vertical motion equation and small-oscillation frequency."),
    sub("C.2. Flat rotation regime"),
    p("For z=0 identify which radial regime of B.3 yields the logarithmic potential and find vc in terms of phi0."),
    sub("C.3. Galactic radial velocity"),
    fig("batch9-galactic-sightline", "Original Sun and emitter geometry, circular radii and longitude ell."),
    p("The Sun is at radius RSun=8.00 kpc. A hydrogen emitter at radius R is seen at longitude ell from the Galactic centre. Both orbit at the same speed vSun. Find emitter radial velocity relative to the Sun, positive for recession, and invert for R."),
    sub("C.4. Three hydrogen sources"),
    fig("batch9-hydrogen-spectrum", "Original radio-frequency spectrum with three peaks and frequency-shift scale."),
    p("At longitude 30 degrees and rest frequency f0=1.42 GHz, use the three radio peaks to find radial velocities to three significant digits and radii as multiples of RSun to two significant digits. Take vSun=220 km/s."),
    sub("C.5. Source positions"),
    p("Sketch possible positions of the three sources in the Galactic plane and explain what changing ell can reveal."),
    sub("D.1. Tully-Fisher exponents"),
    fig("batch9-tully-fisher", "Original six galaxy rotation curves and logarithmic Tully-Fisher data with fitted lines."),
    p("Assume galaxy radius R is independent of mass. Find gamma and eta in \\(M_{tot}=\\eta v_{c,\\infty}^\\gamma\\) from B.3, then estimate the observed exponent from the logarithmic graph."),
    sub("D.2. Outer-galaxy acceleration"),
    p("Estimate Newtonian centripetal acceleration in the outer region of NGC 6946."),
    sub("D.3. Deep-MOND scaling"),
    p("MOND modifies dynamics to \\(\\mathbf F=m\\mu(a/a_0)\\mathbf a\\), with \\(\\mu(x)=x/(1+x)\\). For a circular orbit about mass M and a much less than a0, find the Tully-Fisher exponent and estimate a0 from the galaxy data."),
    sub("D.4. MOND rotation curve"),
    p("For mass M uniformly distributed inside radius Rb, determine vc(r) for all radii with the full interpolation function."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Hydrogen and Galaxies"),
    sub("A.1. Circular electron speed"),
    p("Balance Coulomb attraction and centripetal force: \\(m_ev^2/r=e^2/(4\\pi\\epsilon_0r^2)\\), giving \\(v=\\sqrt{e^2/(4\\pi\\epsilon_0m_er)}\\)."),
    sub("A.2. Bohr orbit scales"),
    p("Use \\(m_ev_nr_n=n\\hbar\\) with force balance. Then \\[r_n=n^2\\frac\\hbar{\\alpha m_ec},\\quad r_1\\simeq5.3\\times10^{-11}\\,\\mathrm m,\\quad v_1=\\alpha c.\\] The precise rounded radius depends on using the supplied alpha=0.00727 or the more accurate fine-structure constant. At the supplied alpha r1 is about \\(5.31\\times10^{-11}\\,\\mathrm m\\)."),
    sub("A.3. Bound-state energy"),
    p("The potential is minus twice the kinetic energy, so \\[E_n=-\\frac{e^2}{8\\pi\\epsilon_0r_1n^2},\\qquad E_1=-\\tfrac12\\alpha^2m_ec^2\\simeq-13.5\\,\\mathrm{eV}.\\] Using the unrounded physical alpha gives the usual -13.6 eV."),
    sub("A.4. Proton-current field"),
    p("The current is \\(ev_1/(2\\pi r_1)\\). The circular-current field is \\(\\mu_0I/(2r_1)\\), hence \\[B_1=\\mu_0e\\alpha c/(4\\pi r_1^2).\\]"),
    sub("A.5. Hyperfine emission"),
    p("The interaction energy is \\(-\\boldsymbol{\\mathcal M}_s\\cdot\\mathbf B_1\\), so \\(\\Delta E_F=2\\mathcal M_sB_1=-4\\alpha^2E_1\\). Use the stated hyperfine factor and \\(\\lambda_{HF}=2\\pi\\hbar c/\\Delta E_{HF}\\) to obtain approximately 0.21 m. The energy difference is positive although the bound-state E1 is negative."),
    sub("B.1. Circular galactic motion"),
    p("Radial force balance gives \\(v_c^2/r=d\\varphi/dr\\), so \\(v_c=\\sqrt{r\\,d\\varphi/dr}\\)."),
    sub("B.2. Visible-bulge mass"),
    p("Outside the bulge, \\(M_b=v_c^2r/G\\). Read approximately 20 km/s at 10 kpc from the red curve. This yields \\(M_b\\simeq1.85\\times10^{39}\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\simeq9.3\\times10^8M_\\odot\\). Graph-reading precision is limited."),
    sub("B.3. Extended density model"),
    p("Spherical integration gives \\[M(r)=4\\pi C_m[r-r_m\\arctan(r/r_m)].\\] Thus \\(k_1=4\\pi GC_m,k_2=4\\pi GC_mr_m\\). At small r, \\(v\\simeq\\sqrt{4\\pi GC_m/3}\\,r/r_m\\). At large r, \\(v\\to\\sqrt{4\\pi GC_m}\\) and \\(M(r)\\simeq4\\pi C_mr\\). The graph plateau near 160 km/s gives \\(C_m\\simeq3.05\\times10^{19}\\,\\mathrm{kg/m}\\). For image radius about 9 kpc the large-r estimate is \\(M\\simeq1.07\\times10^{41}\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\), about \\(5.4\\times10^{10}M_\\odot\\), hence order \\(10^{11}M_\\odot\\). This substantially exceeds the visible-bulge estimate. The source converts 9 kpc incorrectly in one line; using 3.09 times 10 to the 19 metres per kpc fixes it."),
    sub("C.1. Vertical galactic stability"),
    p("\\[\\ddot z=\\frac{2\\varphi_0z}{z_0^2}\\ln(r/r_0)e^{-(z/z_0)^2}.\\] Since the logarithm is negative, the linear force restores z to zero and \\[\\omega_0=\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\varphi_0}{z_0^2}\\ln(r_0/r)}.\\]"),
    sub("C.2. Flat rotation regime"),
    p("At \\(r\\gg r_m\\), \\(d\\varphi/dr=4\\pi GC_m/r\\), so \\(\\varphi=4\\pi GC_m\\ln(r/r_0)\\). Identify \\(\\varphi_0=4\\pi GC_m\\), giving \\(v_c=\\sqrt{\\varphi_0}\\)."),
    sub("C.3. Galactic radial velocity"),
    p("Project the two tangential velocities onto the sightline: \\[v_r=v_\\odot(R_\\odot/R-1)\\sin\\ell,\\qquad R=\\frac{R_\\odot}{1+v_r/(v_\\odot\\sin\\ell)}.\\] Longitudes with zero sine cannot determine R this way."),
    sub("C.4. Three hydrogen sources"),
    p("The peaks have shift magnitudes 0.03,0.15,0.26 MHz. Interpreting the plotted positive shift as the receding Doppler convention yields \\(v_r\\simeq6.33,31.7,54.9\\,\\mathrm{km/s}\\), and \\(R/R_\\odot\\simeq0.95,0.78,0.67\\). A positive received-frequency shift f-f0 would instead mean approach. The source labels its shift inconsistently with its positive-recession velocities. These values use its intended redshift magnitude, not a silent Doppler sign reversal."),
    sub("C.5. Source positions"),
    p("Draw the sightline at 30 degrees from Sun-to-centre, then circles of radii 0.95,0.78,0.67 RSun. Each intersects the line at near and far candidates. Repeated directions trace hydrogen structure, including spiral arms, but simple kinematics alone leaves the near-far ambiguity inside the solar circle. Brightness alone does not uniquely resolve it."),
    sub("D.1. Tully-Fisher exponents"),
    p("Combining \\(M\\simeq4\\pi C_mR\\) and \\(v_\\infty^2=4\\pi GC_m\\) gives gamma=2 and eta=R/G. The plotted slope is about \\((12-9)/(2.6-1.8)=3.75\\), near 4, rather than 2. The printed source fraction reverses numerator and denominator but its numerical value uses this correct slope."),
    sub("D.2. Outer-galaxy acceleration"),
    p("Use \\(a=v^2/r\\). At v=160 km/s and r=5 kpc, \\(a\\simeq1.66\\times10^{-10}\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}\\), decreasing farther out."),
    sub("D.3. Deep-MOND scaling"),
    p("Deep MOND gives \\(a^2/a_0=GM/r^2\\). With a=v squared/r, \\(v_\\infty^4=GMa_0\\), so the exponent is 4. Reading \\(M\\sim10^{10.5}M_\\odot\\) at v=160 km/s gives \\(a_0\\sim1.6\\times10^{-10}\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}\\). The outer data are of this order, becoming more deeply MOND farther out. An acceleration merely comparable to a0 is a crossover rather than asymptotically much less than a0."),
    sub("D.4. MOND rotation curve"),
    p("Let \\(g_N=GMr/R_b^3\\) inside and \\(g_N=GM/r^2\\) outside. The equation \\(a^2/(a+a_0)=g_N\\) has positive root \\[a=\\tfrac12[g_N+\\sqrt{g_N^2+4a_0g_N}],\\qquad v_c=\\sqrt{ra}.\\] This compact piecewise-gN expression covers both regions and matches continuously at Rb. At infinity it approaches \\((GMa_0)^{1/4}\\); at the centre it vanishes, with deep-MOND scaling \\(v_c\\propto r^{3/4}\\)."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2025-t2"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Cox's Timepiece"),
    p("In 1765, British clockmaker James Cox invented a clock whose only source of energy is the fluctuations in atmospheric pressure. Use uniform gravity \\(g=9.8\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}\\), incompressible liquids, no surface tension, constant ambient temperature and isothermal transformations. Atmospheric pressure is uniform with height. Part A uses a thin-walled vertical cylinder, open at the bottom and closed at the top, length H=1 m, area \\(S=10\\,\\mathrm{cm^2}\\), mass m=0.5 kg, in a semi-infinite bath whose surface is z=0. The top has altitude h and internal liquid surface altitude zl. Initially h=zl=0 and no gas is trapped. Lift it slowly until its bottom reaches the bath level. Outside pressure is \\(P_0=10^5\\,\\mathrm{Pa}\\)."),
    sub("A.1. Submerged-tube force"),
    fig("batch9-submerged-tube", "Original three stages of the submerged tube, showing bath level, tube top and liquid level."),
    p("Before vapour appears, find water pressure at the top and upward force F holding the tube."),
    sub("A.2. Three water temperatures"),
    fig("batch9-lifting-regimes", "Original force-versus-lift curves for unbroken and vapour-limited columns."),
    p("For water at 20,80,99 degrees Celsius use respectively densities 1000,970,960 kg/m cubed and saturated vapour pressures 2340,47400,99800 Pa. Determine whether F rises throughout lifting, or levels off at a height hstar. Find maximal force and any accessible hstar."),
    sub("A.3. Mercury vapour correction"),
    p("Replace water by mercury at 20 degrees Celsius, density 13500 kg/m cubed and Psat=0.163 Pa. Find relative error in maximal force if Psat is ignored."),
    sub("B.1. Added mercury mass"),
    fig("batch9-barometric-bulb", "Original two-part mercury tube with bulb and tube lengths and bath level."),
    p("Now neglect mercury vapour. A two-part tube has lower area St and height Ht=80 cm, and top bulb area Sb greater than St and height Hb=20 cm. Let ht be junction altitude. Its holding force is \\((m_{tb}+m_{add})g\\). Identify the mercury volume responsible for madd."),
    sub("B.2. Added mass versus lift"),
    p("At fixed P0 lift from fully immersed to ht=Ht. Sketch madd versus ht, including slopes and breakpoints."),
    sub("B.3. Pressure-driven mass amplitude"),
    fig("batch9-pressure-wave", "Original triangular atmospheric-pressure perturbation, amplitude A and period tau1."),
    p("Fix ht so the liquid is midway up the bulb at mean P0. Pressure perturbation P1 is a triangular wave of amplitude A=500 Pa and period one week. Use St=5 square centimetres and Sb=200 square centimetres. Find mass-variation amplitude while the surface stays inside the bulb."),
    sub("C.1. Motion threshold"),
    fig("batch9-cox-pulleys", "Original clock-model pulley arrangement connecting the cistern, barometric tube and sliding mass M."),
    p("Use the original pulley diagram. A finite cistern and two-part tube hang from cables attached to opposite sides of a mass M sliding horizontally with friction magnitude Fs and stops at x=plus or minus X. Initially x=0 and P1=0 balance. The cistern has area Sc=210 square centimetres, height 30 cm, and the total mercury volume is 5 L. Keep the tube dimensions above. Assume its bottom stays submerged and its liquid level stays inside the bulb. Let \\(\\xi=(S_b+S_c-S_t)F_s/(S_bS_cA)\\), with St negligible compared with Sb,Sc. Find the threshold above which the mass always remains at rest."),
    sub("C.2. Tension at the stop"),
    p("Temporarily block M at x=X and set P1=0. Find the resultant cable tension along x."),
    sub("C.3. Operating regimes"),
    p("For xi below threshold define \\(\\lambda=2(S_b-S_t)\\rho gX/(S_bA)\\simeq2\\rho gX/A\\). Classify the two regimes and sketch x(t)/X over three weeks against the triangular pressure wave. Neglect switching time compared with one week."),
    sub("C.4. Optimal recovered energy"),
    p("In the switching steady regime, W is the frictional work per pressure period. With Sb approximately Sc and negligible St, optimize Fs and X and find Wstar numerically."),
    sub("C.5. Atmospheric work and efficiency"),
    p("Find the atmospheric pressure work in the optimal cycle and the ratio of recovered work to it. A P-V plot may help."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Cox's Timepiece"),
    sub("A.1. Submerged-tube force"),
    p("Hydrostatic balance gives \\(P_w=P_0-\\rho gh\\). The top cap experiences downward pressure difference \\((P_0-P_w)S\\). Thus \\(F=mg+\\rho gSh\\), upward along z."),
    sub("A.2. Three water temperatures"),
    p("Vapour appears when \\(P_w=P_{sat}\\), so \\(h_*= (P_0-P_{sat})/(\\rho g)\\). If this exceeds H, force rises to \\((m+\\rho SH)g\\). At 20 C, hstar=9.97 m, inaccessible, Fmax=14.7 N. At 80 C, hstar=5.53 m, inaccessible, Fmax=14.4 N. At 99 C, hstar=0.0213 m, accessible, and Fmax=mg+S(P0-Psat)=5.10 N. The first two follow behaviour A, the third behaviour B."),
    sub("A.3. Mercury vapour correction"),
    p("The true maximum is \\(mg+S(P_0-P_{sat})\\). Neglecting vapour increases it by \\(SP_{sat}\\), giving \\[\\epsilon=\\frac{SP_{sat}}{mg+S(P_0-P_{sat})}\\simeq1.55\\times10^{-6}.\\]"),
    sub("B.1. Added mercury mass"),
    p("It is the mercury inside the tube above the outside bath surface. Shade that region up to the internal liquid level. The empty tube weight is already included in mtb. Mercury below the bath surface does not contribute added load in the thin-wall model."),
    sub("B.2. Added mass versus lift"),
    p("Let \\(z_*=P_0/(\\rho g)=0.756\\,\\mathrm m\\). The four segments are \\[m_{add}=\\begin{cases}\\rho S_b(h_t+H_b),&-H_b\\le h_t\\le0,\\\\\\rho(S_bH_b+S_th_t),&0\\le h_t\\le z_*-H_b,\\\\\\rho[S_th_t+S_b(z_*-h_t)],&z_*-H_b\\le h_t\\le z_*,\\\\\\rho S_tz_*,&z_*\\le h_t\\le H_t.\\end{cases}\\] Slopes are \\(\\rho S_b,\\rho S_t,-\\rho(S_b-S_t),0\\). They join continuously at 0, zstar-Hb, zstar."),
    sub("B.3. Pressure-driven mass amplitude"),
    p("The internal level changes by \\(P_1/(\\rho g)\\), so added mass changes by \\(S_bP_1/g\\). Its amplitude is \\(S_bA/g=1.020\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\)."),
    sub("C.1. Motion threshold"),
    p("At fixed x, pressure P1 transfers apparent load between containers. Hydrostatic balance and volume conservation give the net tension \\[T_P=\\frac{2S_bS_c}{S_b+S_c-S_t}P_1.\\] Its maximum magnitude is this coefficient times A. Static friction can hold throughout if xi is greater than 2. Thus \\(\\xi_*=2\\). Equality is the marginal threshold."),
    sub("C.2. Tension at the stop"),
    p("The container displacements are opposite because of the cables. Solve equal liquid-level shift and mercury-volume conservation to find the cistern apparent-mass change \\(-2\\rho S_c(S_b-S_t)X/(S_b+S_c-S_t)\\). Its opposite is the tube load change, hence \\[T=\\frac{4S_c(S_b-S_t)}{S_b+S_c-S_t}\\rho gX\\simeq\\frac{4S_bS_c}{S_b+S_c}\\rho gX.\\] It is positive, towards the same stop, not a restoring force."),
    sub("C.3. Operating regimes"),
    p("General tension is \\(T=C_P[P_1+2\\rho g(S_b-S_t)x/S_b]\\), with \\(C_P=2S_bS_c/(S_b+S_c-S_t)\\). Initially motion starts when P1/A exceeds xi/2 and rapidly reaches +X. If \\(\\xi+2\\lambda>2\\), even the most negative pressure cannot release it from that stop, so it stays there. If \\(\\xi+2\\lambda<2\\), it repeatedly switches between the stops. Draw an initial 0 plateau, then +1. In the switching regime it settles to a rectangular wave between +1 and -1 with the forcing period and equal dwell times. The equality is a marginal switching limit. No inertial trajectory is specified by this slow-switching approximation."),
    sub("C.4. Optimal recovered energy"),
    p("The total distance per cycle is 4X, so \\(W=4F_sX\\). The switching boundary is \\(F_s/(S_cA)+2\\rho gX/A\\le1\\). The optimum is approached at \\[X_*=A/(4\\rho g),\\quad F_{s,*}=AS_c/2,\\quad W_*=A^2S_c/(2\\rho g).\\] With the stated values, Xstar=0.945 mm, Fsstar=5.25 N, and Wstar=19.84 mJ. In an exactly quasistatic model the boundary is marginal, so this is the limiting maximum approached from the switching side."),
    sub("C.5. Atmospheric work and efficiency"),
    p("In the equal-area approximation the exposed system volume change is \\(V_1=-S_c[x+P_1/(2\\rho g)]\\). Over the cycle \\(W_{pr}=-\\oint P_1dV_1\\). At optimum switches occur at pressure extrema. The P1-V1 parallelogram has height 2A and horizontal width \\(2S_cX_*\\), giving \\[W_{pr,*}=4AS_cX_*=S_cA^2/(\\rho g)=39.68\\,\\mathrm{mJ},\\quad W_*/W_{pr,*}=1/2.\\]"),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2025-t3"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Champagne Bubbles"),
    p("Fermentation produces dissolved carbon dioxide obeying Henry law \\(c_\\ell=k_HP_{CO_2}\\). Use surface tension \\(\\sigma=47\\times10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{J/m^2}\\), liquid density \\(\\rho_\\ell=1000\\,\\mathrm{kg/m^3}\\), \\(k_H(20\\,{}^\\circ\\mathrm C)=3.3\\times10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm{mol\\,m^{-3}\\,Pa^{-1}}\\), \\(k_H(6\\,{}^\\circ\\mathrm C)=5.4\\times10^{-4}\\) in the same units, \\(P_0=10^5\\,\\mathrm{Pa}\\), ideal-gas exponent gamma=1.3, \\(g_0=9.8\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}\\), and R=8.314 J/(mol K). This problem is a physics model of nucleation, sound and gas expansion. Part A is at T0=20 degrees Celsius, with liquid pressure P0, constant temperature and only CO2 inside the bubbles. Write \\(c_0=k_HP_0\\)."),
    sub("A.1. Bubble pressure"),
    p("Find the pressure Pb inside a spherical bubble of radius a."),
    sub("A.2. Critical nucleation radius"),
    p("The dissolved concentration far away is cl and at the bubble surface cb=kH Pb. Find the critical radius above which growth occurs and evaluate it for cl=4c0."),
    sub("A.3. Growth laws"),
    fig("batch9-bubble-growth", "Original measured bubble-radius growth versus time with axes and error bars."),
    p("For initial radius a0 about 40 micrometres neglect Laplace pressure. The incoming molar flux is either model 1, \\(j=D(c_\\ell-c_b)/a\\), or model 2, \\(j=K(c_\\ell-c_b)\\). Find nc(a), both growth laws, identify the observed model from the original radius-time plot and evaluate D or K."),
    sub("A.4. Rising bubble and viscosity"),
    fig("batch9-bubble-train", "Original bubble train with a one millimetre scale and upward z direction."),
    p("Bubbles detach at frequency fb=20 Hz and rise at instantaneous terminal speed. With Stokes drag \\(6\\pi\\eta av\\), find the main forces and v(a). Estimate viscosity from the original bubble train and millimetre scale."),
    sub("A.5. Radius at the surface"),
    p("With constant growth rate qa, find radius aH after rising height Hl, assuming aH much greater than a0. Evaluate for Hl=10 cm and the graph growth rate."),
    sub("A.6. Dissolved-gas decay"),
    p("For Nb nucleation sites, each emitting at fb, liquid volume Vl and height Hl, find the differential equation and characteristic concentration-decay time. Neglect free-surface diffusion and a0."),
    sub("B.1. Film retraction speed"),
    fig("batch9-burst-film", "Original bubble film retraction stages and rim mass collection geometry."),
    p("A circular hole opens at constant speed vf in a film of thickness h. The exterior film is stationary. Half the released two-surface energy becomes kinetic energy of the rim plus newly collected liquid. Neglect rim-surface growth. Find vf."),
    sub("B.2. Helmholtz frequency"),
    fig("batch9-helmholtz", "Original piston Helmholtz resonator and equivalent open bubble geometry."),
    p("A piston mass mp moves in an aperture of area S above an ideal-gas cavity of equilibrium volume V0 and pressure P0. Small outward displacement z changes volume by Sz. Find frequency neglecting gravity."),
    sub("B.3. Bubble film dimensions"),
    p("For the bursting bubble, \\(V_0=4\\pi a^3/3\\), aperture radius r and \\(m_p=8\\rho_gr^3/3\\), \\(\\rho_g=1.8\\,\\mathrm{kg/m^3}\\). The aperture grows to \\(r_c=2a^2\\sqrt{\\rho_\\ell g_0/\\sigma}/\\sqrt3\\). The maximum acoustic frequency is 40 kHz and bursting time \\(t_b=3\\times10^{-5}\\,\\mathrm s\\). Find a and film thickness h."),
    sub("C.1. Closed-bottle pressure"),
    fig("batch9-bottle-expansion", "Original bottleneck dimensions, headspace and two observed fog types."),
    p("A bottle holds total CO2 amount nT=0.2 mol, liquid volume VL=750 mL and gas headspace VG=25 mL containing only CO2. Find equilibrium pressure at 6 and 20 degrees Celsius."),
    sub("C.2. Expansion temperature and fog"),
    p("For all subsequent parts use Pi=4.69 bar at 6 degrees Celsius and 7.45 bar at 20 degrees Celsius. The gas expands reversibly and adiabatically to P0. Calculate the final temperatures without phase change. Solid CO2 saturation follows \\(\\log_{10}(P_{sat}^{CO_2}/P_0)=6.81-1300/(T-3.49)\\). Identify grey-white water fog or blue CO2-crystal fog for each initial temperature, assuming the two stated fog mechanisms are exclusive."),
    sub("C.3. Cork maximum height"),
    p("At 6 degrees Celsius use Pi=4.69 bar. A cork has mass 10 g, diameter d=1.8 cm, and initially inserted length l0=2.5 cm. Friction is alpha times its contact area. Initially the pressure force barely exceeds friction. Find the maximum rise after exit, neglecting net gas pressure thereafter."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Champagne Bubbles"),
    sub("A.1. Bubble pressure"),
    p("For one liquid-gas interface the Laplace pressure jump is \\[P_b=P_0+2\\sigma/a.\\]"),
    sub("A.2. Critical nucleation radius"),
    p("Growth requires \\(c_\\ell>k_H(P_0+2\\sigma/a)\\). Thus \\[a_c=\\frac{2\\sigma c_0}{P_0(c_\\ell-c_0)}.\\] At cl=4c0, ac=2sigma/(3P0)=0.313 micrometres."),
    sub("A.3. Growth laws"),
    p("The ideal-gas moles are \\(n_c=4\\pi a^3P_0/(3RT_0)\\). Since \\(\\dot n_c=4\\pi a^2j\\), \\(\\dot a=jRT_0/P_0\\). Consequently \\[a^2=a_0^2+2DRT_0(c_\\ell-c_0)t/P_0\\] in model 1, and \\[a=a_0+KRT_0(c_\\ell-c_0)t/P_0\\] in model 2. The measured radius grows linearly at about \\(q_a=0.24\\,\\mathrm{mm/s}\\), selecting model 2. For cl=4c0, \\(K=q_a/(3k_HRT_0)\\simeq1.0\\times10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm{m/s}\\). The source marking range omits its power-of-ten factor in one line."),
    sub("A.4. Rising bubble and viscosity"),
    p("Buoyancy is \\(4\\pi a^3\\rho_\\ell g_0/3\\), downward weight is \\(4\\pi a^3\\rho_g g_0/3\\), and viscous drag is downward. Since gas density is small, \\[v\\simeq2\\rho_\\ell g_0a^2/(9\\eta).\\] Neighbour spacing divided by \\(1/f_b\\) estimates speed at a measured radius. Representative graph measurements a about 0.20 mm and v about 4.5 cm/s give \\(\\eta\\sim2\\times10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{Pa\\,s}\\). Pair the measured radius and spacing consistently."),
    sub("A.5. Radius at the surface"),
    p("Use \\(dz/da=v/q_a=2\\rho_\\ell g_0a^2/(9\\eta q_a)\\). Integrating gives \\[H_\\ell=2\\rho_\\ell g_0(a_H^3-a_0^3)/(27\\eta q_a).\\] Neglecting a0, \\(a_H=[27\\eta q_aH_\\ell/(2\\rho_\\ell g_0)]^{1/3}\\simeq0.40\\,\\mathrm{mm}\\) for viscosity 0.002 Pa s."),
    sub("A.6. Dissolved-gas decay"),
    p("The bubble gas-volume outflow is \\((4\\pi/3)a_H^3N_bf_b\\). Using A.5 this is \\(18\\pi\\eta q_aH_\\ell N_bf_b/(\\rho_\\ell g_0)\\). Convert volume to moles by P0/(RT0) and use \\(q_a=KRT_0(c_\\ell-c_0)/P_0\\): \\[\\frac{dc_\\ell}{dt}=-\\frac{18\\pi N_bf_b\\eta KH_\\ell}{\\rho_\\ell g_0V_\\ell}(c_\\ell-c_0),\\quad\\tau=\\frac{\\rho_\\ell g_0V_\\ell}{18\\pi N_bf_b\\eta KH_\\ell}.\\] It is the excess concentration above c0 that decays exponentially."),
    sub("B.1. Film retraction speed"),
    p("An area increase dA removes surface energy \\(2\\sigma dA\\). Half, \\(\\sigma dA\\), becomes kinetic energy \\(\\rho_\\ell h\\,dA\\,v_f^2/2\\). Thus \\[v_f=\\sqrt{2\\sigma/(\\rho_\\ell h)}.\\]"),
    sub("B.2. Helmholtz frequency"),
    p("Adiabatic pressure is \\(P=P_0[V_0/(V_0+Sz)]^\\gamma\\simeq P_0(1-\\gamma Sz/V_0)\\). Thus \\(m_p\\ddot z=-\\gamma P_0S^2z/V_0\\), giving \\[f_0=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\gamma P_0S^2}{m_pV_0}}.\\]"),
    sub("B.3. Bubble film dimensions"),
    p("Substitute \\(S=\\pi r_c^2\\) into B.2 and eliminate rc. This gives \\[a=\\frac{3\\sqrt3\\gamma P_0}{64\\pi\\rho_gf_{max}^2}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\rho_\\ell g_0}{\\sigma}}\\simeq0.53\\,\\mathrm{mm}.\\] Then rc is about 0.15 mm and \\(v_f=r_c/t_b\\simeq5\\,\\mathrm{m/s}\\). Finally \\(h=2\\sigma/(\\rho_\\ell v_f^2)\\simeq3.7\\,\\mu\\mathrm m\\)."),
    sub("C.1. Closed-bottle pressure"),
    p("Conservation with Henry law gives \\(n_T=k_HP_iV_L+P_iV_G/(RT_0)\\), hence \\[P_i=\\frac{n_T}{k_HV_L+V_G/(RT_0)}.\\] The pressures are about 4.81 bar at 6 C and 7.76 bar at 20 C. These are distinct from the pressures explicitly supplied for the following questions."),
    sub("C.2. Expansion temperature and fog"),
    p("Adiabatic expansion gives \\(T_f=T_0(P_0/P_i)^{(\\gamma-1)/\\gamma}\\). This gives approximately 195 K from 6 C, and 184 K from 20 C. The CO2 sublimation temperature at one bar from the supplied law is approximately 194.4 K. Thus the 6 C case stays just above sublimation and produces the stated grey-white water fog. The 20 C case crosses below sublimation and produces blue CO2-crystal fog. In the multiple-choice wording, statements 1 and 4 are true. Once crystallization occurs, latent heat modifies the no-transition temperature estimate."),
    sub("C.3. Cork maximum height"),
    p("Initial balance gives \\(\\alpha\\simeq(P_i-P_0)d/(4\\ell_0)\\). During extraction at height z, friction is \\(\\alpha\\pi d(\\ell_0-z)\\), with work \\(W_f=-(P_i-P_0)\\pi d^2\\ell_0/8\\). Put \\(\\Delta V=\\pi d^2\\ell_0/4\\). Adiabatic gas work is \\[W_g=\\frac{P_iV_G}{\\gamma-1}\\left[1-\\left(\\frac{V_G}{V_G+\\Delta V}\\right)^{\\gamma-1}\\right].\\] Atmospheric work is \\(-P_0\\Delta V\\). The exit kinetic energy is approximately \\(W_g+W_f-P_0\\Delta V\\), about 0.76 J, ignoring small gravitational work during extraction as in the source. Free-flight rise is this divided by mg0, approximately 7.7 m. Including the cork weight in initial friction balance and extraction changes the result slightly, rather than changing the dominant gas-energy calculation."),
  ],
};
